__d(function(e,t,a,n,s,i,r){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(i,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(i,"WIKI",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return o}});const o={w1l1:"HTTP is stateless by design: once a response is sent, the server retains nothing about the exchange. Every subsequent request must therefore stand entirely on its own, and the server reconstructs whatever context it needs \u2014 who the user is, what they may do \u2014 from the contents of that single request.\n\nThat reconstruction is the quiet root of a large share of web vulnerabilities. The request is authored by the client, which means it is reachable by an attacker. The method, the path, the headers, the cookies, the body: each is a claim, not a fact. A server's job is to decide how much of that claim to believe, and security bugs are almost always a case of believing too much.\n\nThree concrete instincts follow. First, HTTP methods express intent, not authorization \u2014 the server, not the verb, must decide what an action is permitted to do, so treating GET as inherently safe is how state-changing endpoints get abused. Second, status codes are an information channel: a 403 where you expected a 404, or a 500 carrying a stack trace, hands an attacker a map of what exists and how the system is built. Third, identity is re-asserted on every request through forgeable carriers (cookies, tokens, headers), so the entire problem of session security is making a replayable, client-held claim trustworthy.\n\nCarry one question through the rest of the course: for any field in a request, what did the server wrongly take it to prove? Almost every chapter that follows is a specific, weaponized answer to that question.",w1l2:"Request headers feel like environment, but they are input. In a browser they are populated with sensible defaults, which lulls testers into trusting them; drive the same request from a proxy or a script and every header becomes a free-text field the attacker controls. Any decision made on a header's value is a decision made on attacker-supplied data.\n\nThe Host header is the sharpest example. Applications frequently read it to discover 'their own' domain and then build absolute URLs from it \u2014 most dangerously, password-reset links of the form https://{Host}/reset?token=\u2026 . An attacker requests a reset for the victim while sending a Host header pointing at a server they control. The application generates a genuine, valid token and emails it inside a link to the attacker's domain. When the victim clicks, the token is delivered straight to the attacker \u2014 no fake page required. The fix is structural: build absolute links from a server-configured canonical base URL, or enforce a strict allowlist of acceptable Host values, and never echo the request's Host into security-relevant output.\n\nTwo related trust mistakes round out the lesson. The Referer header causes leakage: browsers attach the full referring URL to outbound requests, so any secret carried in a query string escapes to third parties such as analytics and CDNs (and into history and logs); the defenses are a strict Referrer-Policy and keeping secrets out of URLs entirely. The User-Agent \u2014 and any similar self-description \u2014 is freely spoofable, so gating access, features, or security checks on it is bypassed the instant the attacker edits the string.\n\nThe through-line is one rule: a header is input, so derive security-critical values from server state, not from the request's description of itself.",w1l3:"HTTP/1.1 announces how long a message is using text fields \u2014 Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding \u2014 and when those signals disagree, each server in the path must decide how to interpret the bytes. Those judgment calls are the soil that HTTP request smuggling grows in. HTTP/2 was, in part, a fix: it frames messages in binary with explicit, unambiguous lengths, removing the guesswork.\n\nThe trouble is that a real request traverses a chain. An HTTP/2 edge server typically downgrades the request to HTTP/1.1 in order to talk to a back-end origin, and that translation drags the old ambiguity back in. The vulnerable assumption is that the front-end and back-end agree on exactly where one request ends and the next begins. When they don't, bytes the front-end attributes to one request are parsed by the back-end as the beginning of the next \u2014 and the attacker's leftover prefix is prepended to whichever user's request comes through next.\n\nThat prefix is the payload: depending on the target it can capture another user's request, poison a shared cache so other users are served attacker content, or bypass front-end access controls because the back-end now reads traffic through the attacker's framing. The mechanics of building these requests are a later chapter \u2014 Semester 4 covers desync in depth \u2014 but the goal here is to recognize the seam.\n\nThe durable lesson is about boundaries. A protocol upgrade rarely retires the old parser; it layers a new one on top, and vulnerabilities cluster wherever two parsers with different rules meet. Defenders prefer HTTP/2 end to end, normalize or reject ambiguous framing at the edge, and avoid reusing a single back-end connection across different users, which is what lets one user's smuggled prefix reach another.",w10l1:"Command injection is SQL injection's more dangerous sibling. The application builds an operating-system command from user input and passes it to a shell, and the shell \u2014 exactly like the SQL parser \u2014 cannot distinguish the developer's intended command from the attacker's injected syntax. The weapons are shell metacharacters: a semicolon chains a second command, a pipe feeds one command into another, && runs on success, and $(...) or backticks substitute the output of a command. An input such as filename; id therefore smuggles an extra command past the one the developer wrote.\n\nThe reason it ranks among the worst outcomes is that it is remote code execution by the shortest possible path. There is no memory corruption and no multi-stage exploit; there is only text that runs on the server with the application's privileges. Where SQL injection typically steals data, command injection takes the host. It hides anywhere an application shells out \u2014 image and PDF processing, ping or DNS utilities, archive extraction, document export \u2014 so any call to a system binary with a user-influenced argument is a candidate.\n\nThe defense is the same principle as parameterization. Do not assemble a shell string from input; invoke the binary directly with an argument array so there is no shell to interpret metacharacters, validate inputs against a strict allowlist, and remove shell features wherever possible. Separating the command from its data closes the bug at the root.",w10l2:"Template engines such as Jinja2, Twig, Freemarker, and Mako exist to evaluate embedded expressions and stitch the results into output; rendering {{ user.name }} into a name is their ordinary, safe behavior. The danger appears only when user input becomes part of the template source rather than a value passed into it. Concatenating input, as in render(\"Hello \" + name), turns that input into template code the engine will evaluate, whereas render(\"Hello {{name}}\", name=name) keeps it as data. That difference is server-side template injection.\n\nDetection is straightforward and reliable: inject a syntactic expression rather than plain text and observe whether it is computed. If {{7*7}} \u2014 or the equivalent in the target engine's syntax \u2014 returns 49, the engine evaluated the input, which means the attacker is injecting code rather than data. The same probe also classifies the bug: an expression evaluated server-side indicates SSTI, while one returned as literal text on a page where scripts nonetheless execute points to a client-side issue instead. The probe therefore identifies both the existence of the bug and the world it lives in.\n\nThe defensive rule is the template-world form of parameterization: never build a template from user input. User data should be passed as context variables into a static template, so that it is rendered as data and can never be evaluated as an expression.",w10l3:"Confirming an SSTI with a math probe is only the opening move; the objective is to climb from evaluating an expression to executing code on the server. Because the template engine runs inside a real language runtime, the attacker's path is to reach that runtime's dangerous capabilities \u2014 file access and process execution. The standard technique is object traversal: starting from an object the template exposes, the attacker walks the language's introspection facilities (in Python, base classes, subclasses, and builtins) until reaching something that can import a module or run a command. The {{7*7}} result proves evaluation; navigating to a command-execution primitive is the escalation.\n\nEngines differ sharply in how far that walk must go. Some, such as classic Jinja2, advertise a sandbox, but that sandbox has a well-documented history of being escaped through the object graph; others, such as Mako, expose near-complete language access directly. Identifying the precise engine and version \u2014 the work of the previous lesson \u2014 determines which path applies. In a training context this is studied as recognition and reasoning: understanding the shape of the traversal and why it works, not as a reusable weapon. The essential insight is that 'it evaluated my arithmetic' and 'it runs my code' are separated by a single short climb.\n\nDefensively the consequence is that any evaluation primitive should be assumed to become remote code execution. A template engine's sandbox is not a dependable security boundary; the real control is never letting user input become a template, and isolating the renderer if untrusted expressions genuinely must be evaluated.",w10l4:"The defense against server-side template injection is the template-world version of parameterization, and it is the same discipline that fixes SQL injection and command injection. The template is code the developer writes; user input is data passed into it. render(STATIC_TEMPLATE, name=input) is safe, while render(\"...\" + input) is not, because the latter places input into the template source. Keeping input out of the template source is the whole fix.\n\nSeveral practices reinforce it. Preferring logic-less or minimally-logical template engines for anything touching user data means that even in a worst case an injected expression has little to reach, because the template language cannot express arbitrary logic. Crucially, a template engine's built-in sandbox should not be treated as a security boundary, given the history of such sandboxes being escaped; if untrusted expressions genuinely must be evaluated, the evaluator should be isolated at the operating-system or process level rather than behind a library flag. The familiar supporting controls apply as defense in depth: separate the code and data planes, validate inputs, and run with least privilege so a worst-case evaluation reaches as little as possible.\n\nThe throughline of the week unifies all of it. SQL, the operating-system shell, and template engines are three different interpreters exhibiting one identical bug: each is exploited by making input cross from the data plane into the code plane, and each is fixed by keeping input as data \u2014 parameterize the query, pass an argument array to the command, and contextualize the template.",w11l1:"NoSQL databases did not eliminate injection; they changed its serialization. Document stores such as MongoDB express queries as structured objects, and the vulnerability appears when user input is permitted to supply query operators rather than only values. Controlling an operator means controlling the query's logic. The canonical example is a login that builds a query like { username: input1, password: input2 }: if the application accepts a JSON body and the password arrives as { \"$ne\": null }, the comparison becomes 'password not equal to null,' which is true for every record, so the user's input has become part of the query's structure.\n\nA sharper case involves query features that evaluate code directly. MongoDB's $where accepts a JavaScript expression, so input reaching it injects code into the database engine \u2014 NoSQL's own path to code execution. In every variant the root cause is identical to SQL injection: input crosses from the data plane into a place where it is parsed as structure or code. Only the serialization differs \u2014 an object instead of a string of SQL text.\n\nThe defense follows the same logic. Enforce types so that a password field must be a string and can never arrive as an operator object; reject or coerce unexpected structure; avoid code-evaluating features like $where; and use query builders that bind values rather than merging raw user-supplied objects into the filter.",w11l2:"The most common and most severe NoSQL injection is not data theft but authentication bypass. Where a credential check is implemented as a query, an injected operator can make the comparison match without the attacker knowing the secret. Sending the password as { \"$ne\": \"x\" } expresses 'not equal to x' \u2014 true for the real password \u2014 while { \"$gt\": \"\" } expresses 'greater than the empty string,' true for any stored string. In both cases the query returns the user's record despite an incorrect password, and the attacker is authenticated.\n\nRegex operators extend the technique from bypass to extraction. A test such as { \"$regex\": \"^a\" } asks whether a field begins with a given character, so the attacker can read a secret one character at a time \u2014 a blind NoSQL injection that reuses the oracle idea from blind SQL injection, expressed through query operators rather than SQL conditions.\n\nThe class is widespread because many stacks pass the request's JSON straight into the query, and developers picture the password field as a string the user typed rather than as an object the attacker can choose. That mental gap is the vulnerability. The defense is to compare a string against a stored hash rather than running a user-shaped query: enforce the scalar type at the edge, reject objects where scalars belong, and never let the operator in a credential comparison be attacker-influenced.",w11l3:"With four injection variants now in view \u2014 SQL, NoSQL, command, and template \u2014 it is worth recognizing them as a single bug in different costumes. Every injection is the same statement: untrusted input reaches an interpreter, in a context where the input can stop being data and start being structure or code. That observation yields a transferable model rather than a memorized catalogue of payloads.\n\nThe model is three questions asked of any sink. First, which interpreter consumes this input \u2014 a SQL engine, an OS shell, a template engine, an LDAP or XPath processor, even a log parser? Second, what context does the input land in \u2014 a string literal, an operator position, an expression? Third, can the input break out of that context into the surrounding syntax? This is why the techniques rhyme: a UNION clause, a $ne operator, a chained ; id, and a {{7*7}} expression are all instances of 'break out of the data context and add my own structure,' so a query language never seen before reduces to the same three questions.\n\nThe model also predicts severity. An interpreter that can reach the operating system \u2014 the shell, MongoDB's $where, a template engine climbed to its runtime \u2014 implies code execution, whereas one confined to data implies disclosure or authentication bypass; the interpreter's power sets the ceiling. For defenders, the value is that payloads cannot be enumerated but sinks can be examined: at each one, ask whether untrusted input is being parsed where it could become code or structure, and if so, separate the planes there. The model scales where a blocklist cannot.",w11l4:"If injection is a single model, its remedy is a single principle applied per context: never allow untrusted input to be parsed as structure or code, but keep it in the data plane using whatever separation mechanism the interpreter provides. The mechanisms are the same idea translated into each dialect \u2014 parameterized queries for SQL, argument arrays instead of shell strings for the operating system, static templates with context variables for template engines, type enforcement and value binding for NoSQL, and context-aware output encoding for whatever ultimately renders or evaluates the value.\n\nTwo further layers harden the system without ever being the primary fix. Input validation expressed as a strict allowlist shrinks what can arrive, and least privilege shrinks what a successful injection can reach; both are defense in depth behind separation. The portable methodology assembles these into a routine: at each place input is used, name the interpreter and the context, apply that context's separation mechanism, and verify the input cannot break out \u2014 repeating at every sink, including the internal ones that consume already-stored data, which are the second-order trap.\n\nThis closes the server-side half of Semester 2. The same model and the same cure now carry to the client side, where the interpreter is the browser's HTML and JavaScript parser and the contexts are HTML, attribute, JavaScript, and URL. There the bug is called cross-site scripting, and it is the subject of the weeks that follow.",w12l1:"Cross-site scripting is the injection model with the browser as the interpreter. Untrusted input reaches the page in a context where the HTML or JavaScript parser treats it as markup or code rather than text, and attacker script then runs in the victim's session, as the victim. Reflected XSS is the simplest form: input from the request is echoed into the response without encoding, so a script tag placed in a search term is parsed and executed when the victim opens a crafted link.\n\nThe significance of 'as the victim' is the entire point. The script executes in the victim's origin with their cookies and session, so it can act on their behalf, read what they can read, and \u2014 as Week 3 established \u2014 steal any session token not protected by HttpOnly. XSS is code execution inside someone else's authenticated browser.\n\nContext governs everything, exactly as the string-literal-versus-operator distinction governed SQL injection. The same input is inert in one place and live in another: in an HTML body the attacker needs a tag or event handler, inside an attribute they must break out of the quotes, and inside existing JavaScript they close the string first. The defense is correspondingly contextual: output encoding that matches the exact context the value lands in, applied at the sink where the value is rendered, because that \u2014 not the input form \u2014 is where the danger is realized.",w12l2:"Stored, or persistent, cross-site scripting is the high-impact sibling of the reflected form. The payload is saved server-side \u2014 in a comment, a profile field, a support ticket, even a filename \u2014 and then served to everyone who later views it. There is no crafted link to distribute: one injection reaches every viewer. At the sink the bug is identical to reflected XSS, namely input rendered into a page without context-appropriate encoding; what differs is timing and reach, because the payload persists and fires for each visitor.\n\nThe reason stored XSS so often becomes full compromise is the audience it eventually reaches. User-submitted content is frequently reviewed by moderators and administrators in privileged panels, so the attacker's script can execute inside a high-privilege authenticated session rather than an ordinary user's. It also surfaces in unexpected sinks \u2014 a file's name or metadata, a username printed into logs, an order note rendered in a back-office view \u2014 so anywhere stored attacker text is later displayed to a human is a candidate, including the bucket-overwrite scenario from Week 8.\n\nThe defensive rule echoes second-order injection: encode on output, at every place stored data is rendered, in that place's context \u2014 not merely at the input form. The dangerous moment is display, and that display may occur in a different application or view entirely from where the data was first accepted.",w12l3:"DOM-based cross-site scripting occurs entirely within the browser. Client-side JavaScript takes attacker-controllable input and writes it into a dangerous DOM sink, with no unencoded reflection from the server involved at all; the vulnerable code is the page's own script. The structure is a dataflow from a source to a sink: a source such as location.hash, the URL, document.referrer, postMessage data, or localStorage flows into a sink such as innerHTML, document.write, eval, or a script's src, without sanitization in between.\n\nThis makes DOM XSS invisible to server-side defenses. A payload can live in the URL fragment after a #, which browsers never transmit to the server, so server-side output encoding and web application firewalls never observe it; both the bug and its fix reside in the client code. This is precisely why DOM XSS is routinely missed by tools that only inspect server responses.\n\nThe context rules are the same as elsewhere, expressed in JavaScript: assigning to innerHTML parses HTML so a tag or handler runs, passing a value to eval or the Function constructor runs it as code, and setting an element's src or href can execute a javascript: URL. The sink's behavior dictates what the source must contain. Defensively, client code must treat browser-controlled inputs as untrusted, avoid dangerous sinks (preferring textContent to innerHTML and never passing input to eval), and use safe APIs or vetted sanitizers \u2014 the fix lives where the sink is, in the JavaScript, not on the server.",w12l4:"The unifying tool for finding cross-site scripting, and the concept this lesson is named for, is sources and sinks. A source is anywhere attacker-controllable data enters, and a sink is anywhere data is interpreted as markup or code; XSS exists wherever a source reaches a sink without the data being neutralized for that sink. Sources span both the request and the browser \u2014 query and body parameters, headers, the URL and its fragment, postMessage, storage, and stored database fields in the case of stored XSS \u2014 and cataloguing them is the same attack-surface discipline from Semester 1, now aimed at a single bug.\n\nSinks are wherever data becomes active. Server-side, the sink is the point at which a value is written into the HTML response; client-side, the sinks are innerHTML, document.write, eval, a script's src, and event-handler attributes. Each sink sits in a context, and the context dictates the encoding that would neutralize input there. Root-cause analysis is the act of tracing the path: for a suspected XSS, identify the exact source, follow the dataflow to the exact sink, and name the context, then either confirm a missing or insufficient encoding (a real bug) or a correct one (safe). That same trace is the fix, since the remedy is to encode at the sink for its context.\n\nThe defensive principle is that sinks are secured, not sources. Every sink should be inventoried and encoded for its context, or fed through a safe API, and a single source must be assumed to fan out to multiple sinks \u2014 the same value rendered into HTML, into an attribute, and into a script requires three different encodings.",w13l1:"DOM-based cross-site scripting does not live only in raw innerHTML; popular libraries conceal sinks inside innocent-looking calls, and jQuery is the canonical example. In older jQuery, passing a user-controlled value to the selector function \u2014 $(value) \u2014 causes a string that looks like HTML to be parsed as HTML, so the selector itself can run script. The friendlier-named helpers are equally dangerous: .html(x) is innerHTML, .append(), .before(), and .after() parse HTML, and .attr('href', x) can set a javascript: URL.\n\nThe vulnerability is the familiar source-to-sink flow, with a library function as the sink: a value from location.hash or a query parameter reaches $() or .html() without sanitization. Legacy single-page applications built on jQuery are dense with these flows precisely because the sinks do not look dangerous, and a great deal of the web still runs jQuery, so recognizing library sinks is half of finding DOM XSS in real applications.\n\nThe defensive principle extends the source-to-sink discipline to the framework's own API: know the library's sinks as well as the native ones, pass untrusted data as text (.text() rather than .html()), never construct selectors from input, and prefer APIs that do not parse HTML.",w13l2:"Client-side template injection is the browser counterpart of server-side template injection. Frameworks such as AngularJS scan the DOM for template expressions and evaluate them, so when user input lands somewhere the framework treats as a template, double-brace expressions execute in the browser. The detection probe is the same as for SSTI: inject {{7*7}} into a field reflected into a framework-managed region, and a rendered 49 confirms the framework evaluated it client-side \u2014 the input reached the template layer, not merely the HTML.\n\nWhat makes CSTI notable is that it can execute even where classic XSS is blocked. The expression runs through the framework's own evaluator, and historically AngularJS sandbox protections were repeatedly escaped, turning expression evaluation into arbitrary script and sometimes slipping past a Content Security Policy that would have stopped an injected script tag. It is the same context lesson once more: input reached an evaluator \u2014 here a template engine running in the page \u2014 in a context where it is treated as an expression. The interpreter moved into the browser, but the bug is unchanged.\n\nDefensively, user input must not enter framework-evaluated regions as template. Dynamic data should remain in bound values the framework escapes rather than being interpolated into templates, and framework expression sandboxes must not be relied upon as security boundaries.",w13l3:"Modern frameworks \u2014 React, Angular, and Vue \u2014 auto-escape interpolated values by default, rendering {value} or {{value}} as text rather than HTML. That default is the main reason cross-site scripting is rarer in framework applications, and it is also why the remaining bugs cluster precisely at the points where a developer opts out of it. The escape hatches are explicit and pointedly named: React's dangerouslySetInnerHTML, Vue's v-html, Angular's [innerHTML] binding and bypassSecurityTrustHtml. Each declares 'render this as raw HTML,' so untrusted input passed through one is cross-site scripting.\n\nURL contexts are the subtle gap. Frameworks escape HTML, but a value bound into an href or src attribute can still be a javascript: URL, because auto-escaping addresses the HTML context and not the URL-scheme context \u2014 a different sink that the sources-and-sinks model predicts. Consequently the hunt in a framework application narrows usefully: enumerate every escape hatch and every URL or attribute binding, and determine whether untrusted data reaches it. The default path is safe, so the audit targets the opt-outs rather than every interpolation.\n\nDefensively, the escape hatches should be avoided; where raw HTML is genuinely required it should be sanitized with a vetted library first, and URL schemes should be validated to allow http and https while rejecting javascript:. The presence of 'dangerously' or 'bypassSecurityTrust' in code is a smell that warrants review.",w13l4:"A framework's auto-escaping is, at bottom, a trust model: the framework trusts its own developer-written templates and distrusts interpolated data that may be user-controlled, escaping the latter. Cross-site scripting in framework applications is therefore almost always located where a developer overrode that model. Every escape hatch is an explicit assertion of trust \u2014 a declaration that a particular piece of HTML is safe to render raw \u2014 and the bug arises when the vouched-for value is in fact attacker-influenced. The audit follows directly: find each place trust was asserted and check whether the data actually deserves it.\n\nThis is the same trust-boundary instinct that has recurred throughout the course, from Host headers to same-origin-policy exceptions to the cloud 'Authenticated Users' group. Wherever a safe default is deliberately relaxed, that relaxation is the attack surface; frameworks merely express the relaxation as a named function that can be searched for. Client-side template injection fits the same frame, abusing the framework's trust that template regions contain developer expressions by getting user input into a template region \u2014 the same model with a different relaxation, input crossing into the trusted, evaluated zone.\n\nThe defensive conclusion is to minimize trust assertions: keep user data on the distrusted, auto-escaped path; when an escape hatch is unavoidable, sanitize first and document the justification; and review every 'dangerous', 'bypass', or 'trust' call as a deliberate exception, because that is exactly where the next cross-site scripting bug will be.",w14l1:"An uploaded file is not inert if the browser will render it as markup, and the canonical trap is SVG. Because SVG is XML and may legally contain a script element, an upload that passes as an 'image' can in fact be active content that executes when viewed. The danger is realized when user files are served from the application's own origin: an SVG, or an HTML file mislabeled as an image, opened on the application's domain runs script in that origin with the victim's session and cookies, turning a profile picture into stored cross-site scripting.\n\nThe file's bytes are not the only payload surface. Metadata fields such as EXIF and document properties, and the filename itself, are attacker-controlled strings that applications frequently reflect into a page without escaping, making them sinks in their own right. The enabling condition is content-type confusion: a server that trusts the declared type, or that sniffs content and 'helpfully' renders an SVG or HTML file as a page, allows a file that passed an image check to execute \u2014 the check validated the wrong property.\n\nDefensively, user uploads should be served from a separate, cookieless origin or delivered as downloads with Content-Disposition: attachment, accompanied by a restrictive Content-Type and X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, with SVGs sanitized. The governing rule is simply to avoid rendering user files within the trusted origin.",w14l2:"Cross-site scripting runs arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's authenticated origin, so its impact ceiling is whatever the victim can do, and account takeover is the headline outcome. Two routes exist depending on the defenses present. The first is to steal the session: if the session cookie lacks HttpOnly, document.cookie exposes it, and exfiltrating it lets the attacker replay the session as the victim \u2014 which is precisely the route HttpOnly (from Week 3) is designed to block.\n\nThe second route applies when HttpOnly prevents cookie theft, and it is the more important insight: rather than stealing the session, the script uses it. Because the XSS already executes inside the authenticated session, it can read the page's anti-CSRF token and issue privileged requests itself \u2014 changing the account's email, then triggering a password reset to complete a takeover \u2014 without any cookie ever leaving the page. HttpOnly protects the cookie, not the session; an attacker who can act on the victim's behalf needs only to make requests the victim's browser will authenticate automatically.\n\nThe defensive consequence is that HttpOnly is necessary but not sufficient. It must be paired with the real fix of eliminating the cross-site scripting, and with re-authentication (step-up auth) on sensitive actions such as email and password changes, so that a hijacked session alone cannot complete a takeover.",w14l3:"Cross-site scripting does not merely run code; it controls the page's content on the real origin, which makes it an ideal phishing platform. An attacker can inject a convincing login or 're-enter your password' prompt onto the genuine site, served with the genuine URL and a valid certificate. Where the address-bar spoofing and lookalike domains of Week 4 had to defeat the user's origin check, cross-site scripting skips that fight entirely, because there is nothing to spoof \u2014 the content is already on the correct origin that the user, and their password manager, trust.\n\nAs a result, the captured credentials are not even an exfiltration problem the attacker must disguise. A password manager may autofill, the address bar is honest, and the lock icon is real; every signal a careful user checks reports 'safe,' because structurally the page is the real site. The same primitive drives consent and OAuth abuse, since a prompt rendered on the trusted origin can coax a victim into authorizing access or confirming an action with the origin's full credibility behind it.\n\nThis is a further reason cross-site scripting is critical rather than cosmetic: it converts the origin's own trust into a weapon, and there is no client-side 'spot the phish' defense once attacker content renders on the trusted origin. The fix is to remove the cross-site scripting so such content cannot appear there at all.",w14l4:"The quietest weaponization is a resident script that logs keystrokes. A cross-site scripting payload can attach a listener to the page's inputs and stream everything typed \u2014 passwords, messages, card numbers \u2014 to the attacker continuously, while the victim notices nothing. Because the script runs for as long as the page is open, cross-site scripting is not a one-shot event: it can observe, read any data the page can read, issue requests over time, and beacon results outward, behaving as a live agent in the victim's session rather than a single payload.\n\nThis reframes severity. A proof such as alert(1) demonstrates execution but is not the impact; the real impact is full read and write of the page, capture of all input, action as the user, and persistence, which is why a serious cross-site scripting bug is treated as code execution in the victim's browser \u2014 the client-side peer of remote code execution. The week's throughline is that XSS impact equals what the session can do multiplied by how long the script persists: file delivery widens where the payload lands, while account takeover, phishing, and keylogging are what it does, all flowing from the single fact of arbitrary JavaScript in the victim's origin.\n\nThe defensive posture follows. Rate the bug by capability rather than by whether it pops an alert, and keep the defense singular and layered: context-aware output encoding to prevent the injection, Content Security Policy to limit the reach of any survivor, and HttpOnly together with step-up authentication to cap the damage. Prevent first, contain second.",w15l1:"Content Security Policy, introduced in Week 3, is a mitigation, and mitigations are bypassed. A CSP bypass does not break the policy's cryptography or logic; it finds something the policy already permits that will run attacker-chosen script, so the allowlist itself becomes the gadget. The most blatant case is 'unsafe-inline': a policy containing it re-allows inline script, so an injected script tag simply runs and the policy is decorative \u2014 and it appears surprisingly often, added 'temporarily' to ship a feature.\n\nAllowlisted domains are the subtler problem. A policy that trusts a CDN which also hosts a JSONP endpoint, an outdated AngularJS, or an open redirect lets an attacker load their payload 'from' that trusted origin, so a script gadget on an allowed source defeats the allowlist. Scheme and nonce gaps complete the picture: permitting data: or blob: in script-src lets a payload be inlined by URL, and a nonce that is predictable, reused across responses, or reflected lets injected script carry a valid nonce.\n\nA strict policy therefore avoids unsafe-inline, prefers per-response nonces or hashes over host allowlists (where gadgets hide), removes data: and blob: from script-src, and treats every allowlisted origin as code it vouches for. Even then, CSP limits impact rather than removing the bug; it never replaces fixing the underlying cross-site scripting.",w15l2:"DOM clobbering is adjacent to cross-site scripting but requires no script at all. It abuses a legacy DOM behavior in which an element bearing an id or name attribute becomes a property accessible as a global (window.x) or on its parent, so injected HTML can define JavaScript-visible names. The attack injects benign-looking markup \u2014 an anchor or form with a chosen id or name \u2014 that collides with a variable the application reads; an element with id=\"config\" can make window.config resolve to that element instead of the expected object, so the application's logic then reads attacker-controlled values.\n\nIts importance is that it works precisely where script injection does not. A sanitizer that strips script tags but permits anchors, forms, and images still allows clobbering, and Content Security Policy does not stop it because no script executes \u2014 the attacker is rewriting the application's data rather than running code. The classic impact is subverting a security check or redirecting a flow: clobber a variable a guard depends on, such as a flag, a URL, or a configuration object, so the application's own legitimate code makes the wrong decision. It is the confused-deputy idea expressed through the DOM namespace.\n\nDefensively, security decisions should not depend on global or DOM-derived variables; variables should be explicitly declared and scoped, types should be checked (a clobbered global is an HTMLElement, not the expected object), and sanitization must cover id and name attributes, not only script tags.",w15l3:"Mutation XSS is the two-parsers-disagree bug relocated between a sanitizer and the browser. A sanitizer parses untrusted HTML, judges it safe, and emits it; the browser then re-parses that output and mutates it into different, dangerous markup the sanitizer never evaluated. The mechanism is HTML's own complexity \u2014 namespaces such as SVG and MathML, implicit tag closing and reopening, and attribute fixups \u2014 which means a string a sanitizer blessed can become a new tree when assigned to innerHTML, so the 'safe' output round-trips into a script-bearing one.\n\nThe pattern is the recurring one of the course: it is the same disagreement that powered request smuggling (front-end versus back-end) and same-origin-policy bypasses (two origin parsers). Wherever two components parse the same bytes by different rules, the gap between them is the vulnerability. CVE-2020-6802 in Mozilla's Bleach sanitizer is a canonical instance, in which input Bleach considered clean mutated on re-parsing into executable markup \u2014 a sanitizer bypass via mutation rather than an obvious omission.\n\nDefensively, sanitization should use a library that parses with the same engine the browser uses and is explicitly hardened against mutation, kept updated, and treated as defense-in-depth behind context-aware encoding rather than as a guaranteed boundary, because the parser gap continually produces new mutations.",w15l4:"This week surveyed mitigations failing: Content Security Policy bypassed through gadgets, sanitizers defeated by mutation, and script-focused defenses sidestepped by clobbering. The conclusion is not that these controls are useless but that they are mitigations rather than fixes, each surrounded by an arms race. The distinction is sharp: a fix removes the bug \u2014 context-aware output encoding, and not building HTML from untrusted input, means there is no injection to mitigate \u2014 whereas a mitigation assumes the bug might exist and limits the fallout, as CSP caps a survivor's reach and HttpOnly caps cookie theft.\n\nThe operational consequence is that treating a mitigation as a boundary means shipping the actual cross-site scripting and betting everything on the CSP, and this week demonstrates that the bet loses to a determined attacker. Mitigations buy depth and time, not safety. The correct model is layered: prevent the injection with output encoding as the primary control; contain a failure with CSP, HttpOnly, and step-up authentication as the second layer; and harden the helpers \u2014 a current mXSS-resistant sanitizer, a strict CSP \u2014 as the third, with each layer assuming the one before it may fail.\n\nThis closes the cross-site scripting arc, and the same prevent-then-contain discipline carries into the next week's study of CSRF and across the rest of the course: the fix removes the bug, while everything else only limits the damage when prevention slips.",w16l1:"Cross-site request forgery is the dark side of the same-origin asymmetry established in Week 3. Because the browser automatically attaches the victim's cookies to requests \u2014 including those triggered by a different site \u2014 an attacker's page can cause the victim's browser to fire an authenticated request the victim never intended. The browser is the confused deputy: it holds the victim's credentials and obediently uses them for whatever request it is told to make. The attacker does not steal the session; they borrow the victim's browser to act within it, and they act blind, since the send-but-cannot-read rule means they never see the response.\n\nConstruction is straightforward. A state-changing GET can be triggered by an image tag whose source is the action URL, which fires with the victim's cookies the moment the attacker's page loads; a form-encoded POST can be triggered by an auto-submitting hidden form. The defining bug is the assumption that a request arriving with a valid session implies the user intended it \u2014 CSRF breaks precisely that link, leaving the session valid but the intent forged.\n\nThe defense is to require proof of intent the attacker's site cannot supply: an unguessable anti-CSRF token bound to the session, and SameSite cookies that withhold credentials on cross-site requests. The governing principle is that ambient authority \u2014 the mere presence of a cookie \u2014 must never by itself authorize a state change.",w16l2:"Most CSRF findings are not the absence of a defense but a defense that is present yet hollow. The first class is JSON CSRF. An API that accepts a state-changing JSON body is not automatically safe, because a form can be coerced into sending a text/plain body that the server nonetheless parses as JSON; unless the server strictly verifies the Content-Type or requires a token, the cross-site request succeeds. The belief that 'only my JavaScript sends JSON' is enforced by nothing.\n\nToken weaknesses are the second class. An anti-CSRF token protects only if it is unguessable, bound to the user's session, and actually verified on the server. Tokens that are static, predictable, global across users, optional, or accepted-when-present-but-never-checked all collapse to no protection. Leakage compounds the problem: a token placed in a URL escapes via the Referer header (Week 1) and browser history, so it can be harvested and replayed \u2014 a real token belongs in a header or body, never the query string.\n\nDefensively, the token must be verified server-side on every state-changing request, bound to the session, and kept out of URLs; for JSON APIs, the server should require a custom header or a strictly checked Content-Type that a simple cross-site request cannot set. A defense that is never verified is decoration.",w16l3:"The header-based defenses introduced in Week 1 \u2014 validating the Referer or Origin \u2014 hold only when the check is tight, and they fail in predictable ways. A weak regex that merely substring-matches the domain accepts attacker-controlled hosts such as attacker-bank.com.evil.com, and a policy of allowing the request when the header is absent can often be arranged by an attacker from a non-referring context. Subdomain trust is the recurring gap: a check that accepts any subdomain of the target makes a single cross-site scripting flaw or subdomain takeover on a forgotten host \u2014 the Semester 1 problem \u2014 sufficient to pass, so the defense is only as strong as the weakest subdomain.\n\nSameSite cookies have their own edges. SameSite=Lax, a common default, still sends the cookie on top-level GET navigations, so a state-changing action exposed over GET remains forgeable simply by navigating the victim to it, and SameSite=None provides no protection at all. Even SameSite=Strict is not absolute: it is a same-site control, so a payload running in any same-site context, such as a subdomain XSS or a gadget, can issue the request with the cookie attached.\n\nThe defensive posture is therefore layered: validate Origin strictly against an exact allowlist and reject it when required and absent, refuse to trust arbitrary subdomains, set SameSite to Lax or Strict as defense-in-depth, and still require a verified anti-CSRF token \u2014 because each individual control has an edge an attacker can ride.",w16l4:"Multi-stage CSRF chains several forged requests into a single attack \u2014 for instance, using one CSRF-able step to provision or reset state and a second forged request to exploit it \u2014 which demonstrates that protecting only the obvious endpoint is insufficient: every step in a sensitive flow needs protection. It also defeats naive token schemes, because if the request that fetches or sets the token is itself unprotected, or the token is readable cross-site through a misconfiguration, the attacker completes the handshake. A token system is only as strong as the weakest request in the sequence.\n\nViewed from a distance, CSRF is action without proof of intent, riding the ambient authority of the cookie, and it is the mirror image of cross-site scripting on the coin of Week 3's asymmetry: XSS abuses the trust the user's session places in the page, while CSRF abuses the trust the server places in the session's automatically-sent credentials. The real fix is correspondingly singular \u2014 require, on every state-changing request, proof of intent an off-site attacker cannot forge: a verified, session-bound anti-CSRF token, reinforced by SameSite cookies and a strict Origin check. Ambient authority alone must never authorize a change.\n\nThis closes Semester 2. The throughline across the semester is that injection let the attacker control the input an interpreter parses, while CSRF let the attacker control the timing and initiation of requests made under the victim's authority; both are the server trusting something it should not \u2014 the input, or the mere presence of a session.",w17l1:"Path traversal, also called directory traversal, is the filesystem's instance of the injection bug: the application builds a file path from user input and trusts that the input keeps it inside the intended directory. The sequence ../ walks up the directory tree, escaping that confinement to read any file the process can access. The textbook example turns download?file=report.pdf into file=../../../../etc/passwd, and the server reads a file far outside the documents directory because the input was concatenated into a path with no confinement.\n\nThe technique rhymes directly with Week 2. Filters that block the literal ../ are routinely bypassed by encoding (%2e%2e%2f), double-encoding (%252e), or nested sequences such as ....// \u2014 the filter and the filesystem disagree about what the path is, so canonicalization must precede any check. The impact ranges from disclosing source code and configuration secrets such as database credentials and keys, to write primitives when combined with other bugs; reading /etc/passwd is merely the demonstration, while the application's own configuration or private keys are the real prize.\n\nThe defense is not to build paths from input at all. Resolve the path to its canonical absolute form and verify it remains inside the allowed base directory with a prefix check, or map user input to an identifier that selects from a fixed allowlist, so the raw name never reaches the filesystem.",w17l2:"Local File Inclusion is path traversal with a sharper edge: the user-controlled path is passed to an include or require (or a template include), and including a file does not merely read it \u2014 it executes it as code. That distinction, between reading and including, is the distinction between disclosure and remote code execution. The vulnerable shape is include(\"pages/\" + page + \".php\"): setting page to a traversal sequence reads an arbitrary file, while setting it to a file whose contents the attacker controls makes the server run that code. The interpreter from the injection weeks has returned, now reading its program off the disk.\n\nLFI's danger is therefore the path to execution. If the attacker can get attacker-controlled content into any file on disk \u2014 a log, an upload, a session file \u2014 and then include it, disclosure becomes code execution; the techniques are the subject of the next lesson. Even read-only, LFI is severe, exposing source code (which reveals further bugs), configuration containing credentials, and keys, and many real targets sit on a spectrum where an LFI that reads today becomes an LFI that runs once a plantable file is found.\n\nThe defense is to never build an include or require target from user input. A fixed map of allowed pages keyed by an opaque identifier, remote and dynamic includes disabled, and the canonicalize-and-confine discipline from path traversal together remove the bug.",w17l3:"Turning a Local File Inclusion into remote code execution is a matter of getting code where the include can reach it, and the classic methods are a toolkit to recognize rather than memorize, each supplying attacker-controlled bytes the include then executes. Log poisoning is the elegant case: a request field the attacker controls, such as the User-Agent or the request line, is written verbatim into a server log, so embedding code in it and then including the log file causes the engine to execute the logged payload \u2014 the input takes the long way to the interpreter.\n\nPHP wrappers make the include parameter itself the vector: php://filter reads and base64-encodes source, disclosing files that would otherwise execute; data:// inlines a payload directly in the parameter; and php://input feeds the POST body as code, with the scheme acting as the smuggling channel. Other plant sites follow the same idea of a writable, includable location \u2014 an uploaded file even when mislabeled, the PHP session file once an attacker controls a session value, and /proc entries \u2014 which echoes the sources-and-sinks lesson, with the include as the sink and anything writable as the source.\n\nThese techniques are precisely why the claim that 'LFI is only disclosure' is wrong. The defenses are to remove the include-from-input, disable dangerous wrappers (allow_url_include off, php:// restricted where possible), and prevent attacker-controlled content from landing in predictable includable paths.",w17l4:"Remote File Inclusion is the blunt cousin of LFI: where the platform allows including a URL \u2014 as PHP's allow_url_include does when enabled \u2014 the attacker points the include at their own server, and the application fetches and runs attacker-hosted code directly, achieving remote code execution with no plant step. RFI is rarer today because including remote URLs is disabled by default in modern configurations, but where a legacy application or a misconfiguration enables it, it is the most direct form of the bug, since the include's source is literally the attacker's machine.\n\nStepping back, path traversal, LFI, and RFI are a single family: the filesystem and the include treated as an interpreter, with user input crossing into the decision of which file or code to run. It is the injection model once more, with the path serving as structure the attacker should not control. The cure is therefore the injection cure in filesystem clothing \u2014 never construct a path or include target from untrusted input. Map a user-supplied identifier to a fixed allowlist of files or pages, canonicalize and confine any real path to a base directory, and disable remote and dynamic includes.\n\nThis closes the file-system week. The next week takes up the other half of Chapter 6 \u2014 file uploads \u2014 where the attacker does not choose which file to read or run but plants the file itself, the same trust-the-client theme approached from the opposite direction.",w18l1:"File upload inverts the inclusion chapter: rather than choosing which file the server reads or runs, the attacker plants the file itself, and the danger is realized when that file later lands somewhere the server will execute or serve as active content. The headline outcome is a web shell \u2014 a script uploaded into a directory the web server both serves and executes, which, once browsed to, becomes a persistent remote command interface and full remote code execution, delivered through something as innocuous as a profile-photo form.\n\nThe core flaw is trusting client-asserted properties. The browser supplies a filename, an extension, and a Content-Type, and a naive server treats them as facts when they are attacker-chosen strings \u2014 the same lesson as Week 1's observation that a header is input. Severity is therefore decided by the landing zone rather than the file: bytes written inside the webroot with an executable extension are RCE, while the identical bytes stored outside the webroot with a random name and no execution are inert.\n\nThe defensive posture follows: assume the file is hostile and decide its fate by where it goes, not by what it claims to be. Store uploads outside the webroot or on a no-execute, separate origin, assign a server-chosen name, and never allow the upload directory to execute code.",w18l2:"Upload validations fail in a predictable ladder, each rung checking a property that does not actually determine whether the file will execute. Client-side checks are the weakest: JavaScript validating the file in the browser is simply not run when the attacker sends the request through a proxy, so any rule the client enforces is skipped. Extension blacklists leak, because blocking .php misses the many equivalents a server still maps to the interpreter \u2014 .phtml, .php5, .phar, mixed case, trailing dots or spaces \u2014 and enumerating what an interpreter executes is larger than any blacklist.\n\nThe content-based checks are spoofable. The Content-Type is an attacker-set header, so it asserts nothing, and a magic-byte check is defeated by prepending a valid file signature to the payload (GIF89a, then code), producing a polyglot that is simultaneously a valid image and runnable code. Every one of these validations inspects a claim rather than the outcome, which is why the file can pass them all and still execute.\n\nThe reliable approach is therefore not a better claim-check but an allowlist of permitted types verified server-side, combined with making the storage location non-executable, so that even a file which passes every content check cannot run.",w18l3:"When the obvious checks are tight, attackers change the rules of execution rather than the file's label. The first move is to upload a control file: an Apache .htaccess or an IIS web.config that redefines which extensions the server executes, so that a benign-named upload such as image.jpg is run as code. Double extensions and parser quirks exploit how servers map names to handlers \u2014 file.php.jpg may be executed by a misconfigured Apache, and historically a null byte (shell.php%00.jpg) truncated the name before the safe extension \u2014 which is the recurring two-parsers disagreement, here between the validator and the server.\n\nThe deepest class requires no executable file at all: bugs in image-parsing libraries, exemplified by the ImageTragick family, mean that merely processing an uploaded image triggers code execution, with the malformed image as the payload and the thumbnailer as the sink. Consequently, 'it is a real, valid image' is not a statement about safety; a polyglot is both a valid image and a valid script, and a malformed image is a valid exploit for the parser, so confirming the bytes are an image confirms nothing about what processing them will do.\n\nDefensively, control files such as .htaccess and web.config must be disallowed in upload directories, the server should map only the exact intended extensions, and uploaded images should be re-encoded or transformed with a hardened, patched library \u2014 replacing the attacker's bytes with the application's and discarding anything unexpected.",w18l4:"Every upload bypass exploited a check of what the file claims to be, so the defense flips to controlling what happens to the file: its fate is decided by handling rather than by trusting its label. The load-bearing controls store uploads outside the webroot, or on a separate no-execute origin, assign a server-chosen random name rather than the client's, strip any embedded path, and configure the storage so it cannot execute code \u2014 at which point a planted script is merely inert bytes.\n\nValidation should be an allowlist of intended types verified server-side, and transformation is better still: re-encoding images through a hardened library normalizes the format and discards a polyglot's smuggled code, replacing the attacker's bytes with the application's. Serving matters as well, echoing Week 14 \u2014 user files should be delivered with a safe Content-Type, X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, and Content-Disposition where appropriate, ideally from a cookieless origin, so that even an SVG or HTML file cannot run in the trusted context.\n\nThe theme of the week is that uploads are the trust-the-client bug seen from the planting side. The reliable posture assumes the file is hostile and removes its ability to execute or be served as active content, treating claims about its type as advisory at best.",w19l1:"Authentication should reveal nothing about who exists, yet applications leak that fact constantly, and any observable difference between 'valid user, wrong password' and 'no such user' lets an attacker enumerate valid accounts \u2014 a list of real usernames being half of every credential attack. The obvious leaks are messages and codes: 'unknown email' versus 'wrong password', a differing status, a redirect, or a field-specific validation error, and the login, signup ('email already taken'), and password-reset forms all disclose the same fact through different doors.\n\nThe subtler leak is timing. A valid username triggers the expensive password-hash comparison, while a nonexistent one often returns early before any hashing, so the measurable time difference enumerates users even when every message is identical \u2014 a side channel directly analogous to the timing oracle of blind SQL injection. Enumeration matters because it converts blind guessing into targeted attacks: with confirmed accounts an attacker focuses brute force, credential stuffing, and password-reset abuse on real targets and avoids the noise and lockouts of guessing nonexistent ones.\n\nThe defense is to make responses identical for valid and invalid identities in message, status, and timing, performing a dummy hash comparison for unknown users to equalize timing, and returning a generic 'if that account exists, we sent a link' on reset.",w19l2:"Passwords fall to scale. Weak choices and credential stuffing \u2014 replaying username/password pairs leaked from other breaches, which password reuse makes broadly effective \u2014 mean a large fraction of accounts are guessable, so the only real barrier is the rate limit or lockout, and attackers therefore attack the limit rather than the password. Rate limits are routinely bypassable: a limit keyed on client IP is defeated by rotating IPs or spoofing X-Forwarded-For, while a limit keyed on the account is sidestepped by password spraying, trying one common password across many accounts so no single per-account threshold is reached.\n\nFurther bypasses include a counter that resets on a successful step, an endpoint variant that isn't limited such as a mobile API or legacy login, and race conditions that fire many attempts before the counter updates; the limit is only as strong as its weakest-protected path. Account lockout carries its own trap, because locking on failed attempts lets an attacker lock out every user as a denial of service, so the design must throttle without becoming a weapon, and throttling is generally safer than a hard lockout.\n\nThe defensive posture is to rate-limit on a server-trusted identity rather than a spoofable header, apply it uniformly across every authentication endpoint, prefer throttling plus step-up controls (MFA or CAPTCHA after failures) over lockout, and assume breached-password reuse by blocking known-breached passwords and offering MFA.",w19l3:"CAPTCHA exists to prove that a human is acting, gating the brute force of the previous lesson, and like every control in this course it fails when the implementation proves the wrong thing or can be skipped. The classic flaw is replay: if a solved CAPTCHA token is accepted more than once, or is not actually tied to the specific request, an attacker solves the puzzle a single time and replays that token across thousands of automated attempts, because the proof was never bound to the action.\n\nEnforcement gaps are equally common \u2014 a CAPTCHA validated only in client-side JavaScript can be skipped via a proxy, one required on the web form but absent from the API endpoint is simply avoided, and one checked only on the first attempt is irrelevant thereafter, so automation uses whichever path does not ask. Weak challenges fall to automation directly, since low-quality or static image CAPTCHAs are solved by OCR and any CAPTCHA can be funneled to a human or machine-learning solving service at low cost; and if the expected answer is ever sent to the client, the protection is void.\n\nThe defense is to generate a fresh, single-use challenge bound to the specific request, verify it server-side on every protected action, never leak the answer, and treat CAPTCHA as friction layered with rate limiting and MFA rather than as a standalone barrier.",w19l4:"Password reset is a second front door to every account and is frequently weaker than the first, because it must both authenticate the requester and deliver a one-time secret, and each step has a well-worn failure. Token weakness is the core problem: a reset token that is predictable (sequential, timestamp-based, or short), not bound to the account, reusable, or non-expiring can be forged or replayed, so the token must be long, random, single-use, time-limited, and tied to exactly one account.\n\nHost-header poisoning returns from Week 1: when the application builds the reset link from the request's Host header, an attacker triggers a reset for the victim and the email's link points at the attacker's domain, delivering the victim's valid token to the attacker; links must therefore be built from a server-configured canonical host. Flow flaws complete the picture \u2014 leaking the token in the response or via the Referer header, allowing the email or identity to be changed mid-flow, skipping rate limits on the reset endpoint so the token can be brute-forced, or failing to invalidate the token after use \u2014 each of which turns account recovery into account takeover.\n\nThe defensive principle is to treat reset as authentication: use strong single-use expiring tokens bound to the account, deliver them via a server-configured canonical URL rather than the Host header, rate-limit the endpoint and avoid leaking the token, invalidate it on use, and consider step-up verification for sensitive accounts. This closes the authentication week.",w2l1:"Encoding is the act of re-representing bytes so they survive a particular channel \u2014 percent-encoding for URLs, entities for HTML, Base64 for binary-in-text, escapes for Unicode. None of it changes what a value means; it changes how the value is written. Security trouble starts when the written form and the meaning drift apart as the value travels through a request, because the code that inspects the value and the code that acts on it can end up looking at different forms.\n\nThe defining mistake is validate-then-decode. A filter runs at one point and judges the bytes it can see there; if any later stage decodes those bytes, the filter evaluated a disguise rather than the payload that reaches the sink. The attacker's job is simply to encode a blocked payload just enough that the filter's pattern no longer matches, then rely on a downstream decode \u2014 in the framework, the database driver, the template engine, the file API \u2014 to restore it before it is used.\n\nThe fix is to invert the order: canonicalize first. Fully decode the input to a single canonical form for the context it is headed to, validate that canonical form, and only then use it. Equally important, refuse to act on input that is still encoded after canonicalization, which is a strong signal that someone is trying to smuggle a form past your checks.\n\nHold this as the lens for the whole week: the bug is never the encoding itself, it is the gap between the filter's view of the data and the sink's view. Close that gap and the rest of the week's tricks stop working.",w2l2:"Percent-encoding writes a byte as %XX, and every component that handles a URL \u2014 the browser, a proxy, a load balancer, the application framework \u2014 may decode it. The decisive detail is how many times a given value is decoded along the path, and whether the component that validates it decodes the same number of times as the component that ultimately consumes it. When those counts differ, a filter can be made to inspect a value at one decode depth while the sink reads it at another.\n\nDouble encoding weaponizes that difference. Encode the payload once, then encode the result again: a dot becomes %2e, and %2e becomes %252e. A filter that decodes a single time and validates sees %2e%2e%2f, which is not the string '../' it was told to block, so it passes the request. A downstream component then decodes a second time, turning %2e back into a dot, and the path traversal it was supposed to stop is reconstructed after the check has already succeeded.\n\nThe same shape appears beyond path traversal \u2014 blocked keywords, protocol identifiers, and routing tokens can all be hidden one extra layer deep when a control assumes a fixed decode depth. The attacker simply adds one more layer than the control removes.\n\nThe defense is to decode to a fixed point: keep decoding until the value stops changing, and validate only that fully-resolved form. Alternatively, reject any input that is still encoded after a single canonical decode. Either way, the rule is to validate at the same depth the sink consumes, so the filter and the sink can never disagree about what the value is.",w2l3:"The previous lessons established one gap \u2014 validate in one form, consume in another. This lesson widens it: a single logical character has many legal spellings, and each consuming context decodes its own. A less-than sign can be written as &lt;, &#60;, &#x3c;, %3C, or a Unicode escape, and a blocklist that recognizes one of those is blind to the others.\n\nHTML entity decoding is the canonical example. A server that scans response or input text for the literal string <script> will not find it in &#x3c;script&#x3e;, but the browser's HTML parser decodes the entity form back into a real tag at parse time \u2014 precisely after the server's check has passed. Base64 generalizes the idea to whole payloads: a blocklist staring at a Base64 string sees only opaque text, so an application that decodes the value and forwards the bytes to a sink without re-validating them has inspected ciphertext rather than the real input. Unicode is the most subtle case, because normalization forms such as NFKC and case-folding map distinct code points onto the same character after validation has run; a string that passed a check can normalize into a dangerous one downstream, and two strings that look different can be treated as equal.\n\nAcross HTML, Base64, and Unicode the defense does not change. Decode or normalize the value to a single canonical form for the context it is bound for, validate that form, and re-validate anything you decode before it reaches a sink. The encodings are just different alphabets for the same underlying mistake, and canonicalization-before-use is the single rule that closes all of them.",w20l1:"Two questions are constantly confused, and the gap between them is an entire vulnerability category. Authentication asks 'who are you?' and authorization asks 'are you allowed to do this?'; logging a user in answers only the first and says nothing about the second \u2014 the lesson already met in Week 8, that 'authenticated' is not 'authorized.' Broken access control is the failure to answer the second question on every action and every object, and it is consistently the most common and highest-impact class precisely because authorization is not a single check but one that must be repeated correctly at every endpoint and resource.\n\nThe shape is uniform: a user authenticates, then requests something they should not be able to reach \u2014 another user's record, an admin function, a different tenant's data \u2014 and the server, having confirmed they are logged in, performs it without asking whether they are permitted. The identity was valid; the permission was never checked. It hides well because the happy path looks correct: the interface only offers allowed actions, so testing through the UI never triggers the missing check, and the bug lives in the requests the UI does not make but the server still honors.\n\nThe defense is to authorize every request server-side at both the function level (can this role call this endpoint?) and the object level (does this user own this specific record?), deny by default, and never treat the UI hiding an action as the control.",w20l2:"Insecure Direct Object Reference is the most common form of broken access control. The application references an object by a client-supplied identifier \u2014 /account?id=123, /api/orders/456 \u2014 and fetches or modifies it without checking that the requester is allowed that specific object. The attack is exactly as simple as it sounds: change the identifier, so id=123 becomes id=124 and returns someone else's account, or order 456 becomes 457 and reads a stranger's invoice. The server confirmed the user is logged in and then trusted the identifier to point only at their own data.\n\nThe missing control is a per-object ownership check \u2014 does the current user own, or hold a role permitting, this exact object? Authentication established who the user is; IDOR is forgetting to ask whether this record is theirs. Predictable identifiers such as sequential integers make exploitation trivial, but unpredictable identifiers like UUIDs are not a fix, only obscurity: a guessed, leaked, or enumerated UUID still works if the ownership check is absent, because the identifier's randomness is not authorization.\n\nThe defense is to enforce an ownership or permission check on every object access, server-side and scoped to the authenticated user \u2014 for example querying orders WHERE id = ? AND owner = current_user \u2014 and to treat object references as untrusted input rather than as proof of entitlement.",w20l3:"Web parameter tampering is the practice of trusting client-supplied values for decisions the client should not make. A hidden field such as price=9.99, a role=user parameter, an isAdmin=false flag, or a userId in the request body are all attacker-editable, so if the server acts on them rather than deriving the equivalent value server-side, the attacker simply changes them \u2014 lowering a checkout price, flipping a role or privilege flag, or swapping an identifier to act as someone else. A price, a header, and a hidden field are all just input, the Week 1 lesson once more.\n\nForced browsing is the complementary failure: privileged functionality that is 'protected' only by not being linked in the interface. Endpoints like /admin, /export, or /internal exist and function, but the navigation never surfaces them, so requesting the URL directly reaches the function. This is security by not-linking, which is no security at all, echoing the content fuzzing of Semester 1. Both flaws share IDOR's root cause: the server trusts the client to stay in bounds \u2014 to send honest values and to visit only linked pages \u2014 and authorization that depends on the client behaving is not authorization.\n\nThe defense is to derive security-relevant values server-side (price from the catalog, role from the session, identity from the authentication context, never from the request body) and to enforce authorization on every function regardless of whether it is linked, denying by default.",w20l4:"Access-control failures break in two directions, and naming them sharpens analysis. Horizontal failures access another user's data at the same privilege level \u2014 IDOR, changing whose object is touched \u2014 while vertical failures gain a higher privilege level, calling admin functions or flipping a role, which is privilege escalation. Both are the same missing decision asked along different axes: may this subject perform this action on this object? Horizontal attacks vary the object's owner; vertical attacks vary the action's required privilege, so identifying which axis a finding crosses classifies it.\n\nThe unifying model is subject-action-object: every request should produce a server-side authorization decision over who is asking, what they are doing, and which resource is involved. Broken access control is that decision being absent, incomplete \u2014 checking the function but not the object, or the reverse \u2014 or made from client-controlled input. The fix is therefore architectural rather than per-bug: enforce authorization in one central place that every request passes through, deny by default so that the absence of a rule means no access, derive the subject from the authenticated session, and check both the action's required role and the object's ownership. This is the Week 8 lesson made concrete \u2014 'authenticated' was never 'authorized.'\n\nThis closes the access-control week and the identity arc so far. The next week goes inside the credential the session rides on \u2014 JSON Web Tokens \u2014 where the question shifts from whether the check is present to whether the token itself can be forged.",w21l1:"A JSON Web Token moves session state to the client: rather than maintaining a server-side session keyed by an opaque cookie, the server issues a self-contained token carrying the claims \u2014 user id, role, expiry \u2014 and trusts it back on each request. That statelessness is the design's appeal and the entirety of its risk. Structurally a JWT is three base64url-encoded parts, header.payload.signature: the header names the signing algorithm, the payload holds the claims, and the signature is computed over both with a secret or key. The crucial detail is that base64url is encoding, not encryption, so anyone holding the token can read and edit the header and payload.\n\nConsequently the signature is the only thing standing between an attacker and a forged claim such as 'role: admin', and every JWT attack targets that single fact: either the server fails to verify the signature properly, or it verifies with a key the attacker can obtain or guess. Two consequences follow immediately. Secrets must never be placed in a JWT payload, because the holder can read it, and the server must verify the signature on every request and reject anything that does not validate, since an unverified JWT is merely attacker-supplied JSON.\n\nThe defensive posture is to treat the token as untrusted input until the signature verifies: pin the expected algorithm, verify with a strong secret or the correct public key, validate claims such as expiry, issuer, and audience, and remember that the payload is public.",w21l2:"The JWT header names the signing algorithm, and the original specification includes alg: \"none\" \u2014 an 'unsecured' token carrying no signature. A naive library or implementation that honors the header's stated choice will accept a none-algorithm token as valid, having verified nothing. The attack follows directly: take a valid token, change the payload to forge claims such as 'role: admin', set the header's alg to none, and remove the signature entirely; a verifier that reads the algorithm from the attacker-controlled header and finds none treats the forged claims as authentic.\n\nThe root flaw is letting the token decide how it is verified. The header is part of the attacker's input, so trusting it to select the algorithm \u2014 including the option not to verify at all \u2014 is the same confused-deputy mistake as every other trust-the-client bug in the course. It is also the cleanest demonstration of JWT's core truth: because the signature is the only trust, an attack that removes the signature requirement is total, needing no secret and no cracking, only an unsigned token the server forgot to reject.\n\nThe defense is to never accept alg=none for authenticated tokens and never to let the token's header choose the verification algorithm; the expected algorithm is pinned server-side and anything else is rejected, because verification is the server's decision rather than the token's.",w21l3:"Even when the server verifies the signature, two key-related attacks remain. The first concerns symmetric signing: HS256 uses a shared secret, and if that secret is weak \u2014 a dictionary word, a default, or a short string \u2014 an attacker cracks it offline, using the token itself as a verification oracle, and thereafter signs any forged token at will. The second is algorithm confusion, the more elegant case. Asymmetric signing with RS256 verifies using the RSA public key and signs with the private key; an attacker changes the header's algorithm to the symmetric HS256 and signs a forged token using the public key's bytes as the HMAC secret.\n\nThat works because the public key is, by definition, public: if the verifier is implemented to use 'the configured key' for whatever algorithm the header names, it will HMAC-verify with the public key the attacker also possesses, and the forgery validates. As with alg=none, the underlying bug is trusting the header to choose the algorithm. Both attacks reduce to JWT's single truth from a different angle \u2014 the signature is only as good as the secrecy and correctness of the key \u2014 so a guessable HMAC secret or a public key misused as an HMAC secret hands the attacker the ability to sign.\n\nThe defenses are to use long, random, high-entropy HMAC secrets or proper asymmetric keys, to pin a single expected algorithm and reject mismatches (which eliminates confusion), and to verify with the key type the algorithm requires rather than a one-key-fits-all verification call.",w21l4:"Even a perfectly signed token can be mishandled, because session security also concerns where the credential lives, how long it lasts, and whether it can be revoked \u2014 and JWTs make some of these harder precisely because they are stateless. Storage is a deliberate tradeoff that returns to Week 3: in an HttpOnly cookie the token is unreadable by script (XSS-resistant) but auto-sent by the browser (CSRF-exposed, requiring CSRF defenses), while in localStorage it is not auto-sent but is reachable by any XSS. There is no free option, so the defender picks the threat they defend and covers the complement.\n\nRevocation is the JWT-specific trap: a stateless token is valid until it expires, so logging out or banning a user cannot instantly invalidate it without reintroducing server-side state \u2014 a denylist, or short-lived tokens paired with refresh \u2014 and a long-lived stateless token means a stolen token stays good. Two further classics apply: a short expiry limits the window of a stolen token, and session fixation, in which an attacker fixes a known session identifier before the victim logs in and then inherits the authenticated session, is defeated by rotating the session or token on privilege change at login.\n\nThe defensive posture is to choose storage for the threat model and cover the complement (HttpOnly with a CSRF token, or disciplined XSS hygiene), keep tokens short-lived with refresh, maintain server-side revocation for logout and ban, and rotate on login. Signing correctly is necessary but is not the whole job.",w22l1:"OAuth lets a user log in to an application (the client) through an identity provider (the IdP) \u2014 the familiar 'Sign in with X.' The IdP authenticates the user and redirects back to the client's registered redirect_uri carrying an authorization code or token, and that redirect is precisely where the secret crosses and where it is stolen. The core flaw is loose validation of redirect_uri: if the IdP accepts it by substring or prefix, allows any subdomain, or the registered domain contains an open redirect, an attacker can craft a flow that delivers the victim's code to an attacker-controlled URL.\n\nThe attack sends the victim an OAuth link whose redirect_uri the IdP wrongly accepts but which lands on the attacker; the victim authenticates legitimately, the IdP redirects with the code, and the code arrives at the attacker, who exchanges it and becomes the victim \u2014 and, as Week 1 noted, secrets in URLs also leak through the Referer header. Related slips compound it: a missing or unverified state parameter (OAuth's anti-CSRF nonce) allows flow fixation, and over-broad scopes turn a minor consent into broad access.\n\nThe defense is to validate redirect_uri by exact match against a registered allowlist with no substrings or wildcards, keep the registered domain free of open redirects, require and verify state, and request least-privilege scopes, so the redirect can only ever land where it was registered.",w22l2:"SAML is XML-based single sign-on in which the identity provider sends the service provider a signed XML assertion vouching that the bearer is a particular user, so the service provider's entire security rests on verifying that signature \u2014 the same principle as a JWT, expressed in a far more complex format. Signature exclusion is the bluntest break: where the service provider verifies a signature only when one is present, an attacker strips the signature and submits an unsigned assertion claiming an administrative identity, and the verifier, finding nothing to check, accepts it \u2014 the SAML cousin of alg=none.\n\nXML Signature Wrapping is the subtler attack: XML's complexity lets an attacker retain the original signed element so the signature still validates, while adding a second, malicious assertion positioned so the application reads it instead, with the signature checking one element and the application trusting another. Both are the recurring two-parsers disagreement, in which the component verifying the signature and the component reading the claims disagree about which bytes matter, and the format's complexity \u2014 namespaces, references, canonicalization \u2014 works in the attacker's favor.\n\nThe defenses are to require a signature and reject anything unsigned, to verify that the signature covers exactly the assertion element whose claims are consumed, to resolve and validate references strictly, and to rely on well-audited libraries with wrapping protections rather than hand-rolled XML signature verification.",w22l3:"Multi-factor authentication is meant to ensure that a stolen password is insufficient, but implementations leak in characteristic ways. The most common flaw is also the simplest: an OTP that is not rate-limited reduces a six-digit code to a million possibilities that are brute-forced in minutes, and a second factor that can be guessed is not a factor. Worse is the flow flaw in which the MFA step is merely cosmetic \u2014 if the endpoint reached after the password already issues the authenticated session, or the protected resource can be requested directly while skipping the code page, the factor is never actually enforced, existing on the screen rather than on the server.\n\nReplay and reuse extend the problem: an OTP accepted more than once, or valid long after issue, allows a captured code to be reused, and 'remember this device' tokens that are guessable or not bound to the device become a permanent MFA skip. Recovery is the soft underbelly, since weak or unlimited backup codes, codes emailed in cleartext, and reset flows that disable MFA let an attacker route around the factor entirely; as with password reset, recovery must be as strong as the thing it recovers.\n\nThe defenses are to rate-limit and expire single-use OTPs, enforce the factor server-side on the state transition rather than by displaying a page, bind and limit 'remember device' tokens, and harden recovery to the same bar as MFA itself.",w22l4:"Web cache deception tricks a shared cache into storing a victim's private, authenticated page where an attacker can read it. The classic form requests a path such as /account/info.css: the origin ignores the spurious extension and returns the victim's account page, while the CDN or cache sees '.css', assumes a static asset, and caches the response, after which the attacker simply fetches that URL and receives the cached private page. The root cause is the now-familiar disagreement \u2014 the cache decides cacheability by one rule (the extension or path) while the origin generates content by another (the route) \u2014 so the two components disagree about what the response is and whether it is safe to store.\n\nThis generalizes the entire week. OAuth's redirect, SAML's signature, MFA's step, and the cache's rule are all handoffs between parties \u2014 IdP and client, signer and reader, factor and session, cache and origin \u2014 and the bug is consistently one party trusting a value or a view that another party controls or computes differently. Federation and delegation therefore multiply the trust boundaries: more parties mean more handoffs and more places two components can disagree about identity, authorization, or cacheability, so the complexity is itself the attack surface.\n\nDefensively, caching requires explicit cache-control directives that extension or path heuristics must never override for authenticated responses, and federation requires validating every handoff value \u2014 redirect_uri, signature coverage, the enforced factor \u2014 while assuming the other party's view may differ from one's own. This closes the identity-and-access arc, and the next week turns to business logic.",w23l1:"Business logic flaws are a distinct species of vulnerability, because there is no malformed input, no injection, and no broken authentication check involved. Every request is well-formed and individually permitted; the bug is that the application's rules fail to enforce an assumption the developer relied on, so a legitimate-looking sequence produces an illegitimate outcome. The characteristic shape is 'valid request, wrong result' \u2014 buying an item for a negative price, redeeming a one-time coupon twice, skipping the payment step, withdrawing more than the balance \u2014 where nothing is technically wrong with any single request and the business rule they collectively violate was simply never checked.\n\nThis is precisely why scanners cannot find them. Automated tools look for technical signatures, such as a quote that breaks a query or a script that executes, but a business logic flaw has no signature: it is a perfectly normal request that the application should have refused on policy grounds. Finding them is therefore a modeling exercise \u2014 learn what the feature is supposed to guarantee (you cannot spend money you do not have, a coupon applies once, steps happen in order), then look for inputs or sequences the code permits that break that guarantee. The bug lives in the gap between intent and enforcement.\n\nDefensively, every business invariant must be enforced explicitly on the server: quantities are positive, totals are recomputed server-side, state transitions are validated, and one-time actions are tracked. The application cannot assume that the client, or even the happy path, will respect a rule it did not actually code.",w23l2:"The most common business logic flaws concern numbers the application handles naively. A quantity field that accepts negatives turns a purchase into a credit, and an amount permitted to go negative lets a transfer add money rather than move it; in both cases the invariant 'this value must be positive' was assumed rather than enforced. Price and total manipulation overlaps with the parameter tampering of Week 20 \u2014 if the client sends the price or total, the attacker sets it \u2014 and even where the server recomputes, naive arithmetic can be gamed by applying a percentage discount to a negative quantity or by exploiting rounding so that many tiny transactions net free value.\n\nDiscounts and coupons are a logic minefield: stacking multiple single-use codes, reapplying one across requests, combining a referral credit with a refund, or racing two redemptions (the subject of the next week). Each is a rule \u2014 'one coupon, once' \u2014 that the code did not actually guarantee. Currency and rounding bugs are subtler still, arising from mixing currencies without conversion, rounding in the attacker's favor at each step, or precision errors accumulated across many operations, so that each operation is individually correct while the financial invariant is violated overall.\n\nThe defense is to enforce numeric invariants server-side: reject non-positive quantities and amounts, recompute every price and total from trusted server data, apply discounts under explicit one-time rules, and handle money with proper decimal types and consistent currency \u2014 never trusting a client-supplied value or a naive recomputation.",w23l3:"Multi-step workflows \u2014 checkout, registration, identity verification, a funds transfer \u2014 encode a state machine that the developer imagines users walking through in order, and the flaw appears when the server does not enforce that machine, allowing the attacker to take a path the developer never drew. The classic case is step-skipping: jumping straight to 'confirm order' without paying, reaching a download without purchasing, setting a new password without proving the reset, or landing on the post-checkout success URL directly; if a later step does not verify that the earlier ones completed, it simply executes.\n\nReordering and replay extend the problem. Performing steps out of sequence can bypass a check that only runs in the intended order, and replaying a step \u2014 submitting the order twice, applying an approval again \u2014 succeeds when the server does not track that the step already happened. Each is a state transition the code permitted but should not have, and the root cause is identical to access control and IDOR: the server trusts the client to follow the intended flow, just as it earlier trusted the client to send honest values and to visit only linked pages. A workflow that depends on the client behaving is not enforced.\n\nThe defense is to model the flow as an explicit server-side state machine in which each step verifies the required prior state, transitions are validated, and completed one-time steps are recorded so they cannot be skipped, replayed, or reordered \u2014 the server, rather than the UI's page sequence, owning the workflow.",w23l4:"The subtlest business logic flaws break no single rule, using features exactly as designed in a combination or context the designer never imagined \u2014 a refund flow combined with a slow shipping-cancel that lets the attacker keep the item, a loyalty bonus that triggers on transfers between the attacker's own accounts, a gift that can be sent to oneself. Each feature is correct in isolation; their interaction is the bug. This is why logic testing is creative rather than mechanical: the goal is emergent behavior across features, found by asking what a guarantee assumes and what happens if it is combined with another feature, run at an unexpected time, or aimed at an unexpected target.\n\nThe method is to make the implicit explicit by enumerating the assumptions and invariants the application relies on \u2014 money is conserved, a benefit is granted once, a role cannot act on itself, a resource cannot be in two states \u2014 and then deliberately probing each for an input, sequence, or combination that violates it. This ties the whole semester together: files, identity, and logic were each the server trusting the client to stay in bounds \u2014 of the filesystem, of its permissions, and now of the intended way to use the features \u2014 and business logic is that trust at its most abstract, trusting users to use the application as imagined.\n\nDefensively, the invariants should be written down and enforced centrally and explicitly, feature interactions threat-modeled rather than only individual endpoints, and it should be remembered that 'the user did only allowed things' is not a defense when the allowed things combine into harm. The next week turns to race conditions, where the timing of allowed actions becomes the exploit.",w24l1:"A race condition is a timing bug: code checks a condition and then acts on it, assuming nothing changed in between, but if two requests run concurrently both can pass the check before either commits its change. That window between check and use is the vulnerability, captured by the term TOCTOU \u2014 time-of-check to time-of-use. The mental model is the statement 'if balance is at least ten, subtract ten,' which is correct once but, run as fifty simultaneous copies, lets all of them read a balance of ten, pass the check, and subtract, because none had committed when the others looked.\n\nThis is a different axis from every prior vulnerability. Earlier flaws concerned what an attacker sends \u2014 input, identity, sequence \u2014 whereas a race concerns when: the same allowed action fired in parallel, exploiting the fact that the server processed requests as though they were sequential when they were not. It appears wherever a check-then-act touches shared state without atomicity \u2014 balances, inventory, one-time coupons, vote counts, rate limits, used-already flags \u2014 so any time the read, the decision, and the write are not a single indivisible operation, a window exists.\n\nThe defense is to make check-and-act atomic: a database transaction with proper locking, an atomic conditional update such as UPDATE ... WHERE balance >= 10, or an idempotency key collapses the window so that concurrent requests cannot all pass the same stale check.",w24l2:"The headline race is limit bypass: an action the application allows up to some limit \u2014 withdrawing up to a balance, redeeming a coupon once, casting one vote, claiming a signup bonus \u2014 is fired in many simultaneous copies so they all clear the limit check before it updates. The limit was real, but concurrency made it count once for many requests. Money is the obvious target: ten parallel withdrawals of 100 against a 100 balance can remove 1000 if each reads the original balance before any debit commits, a double-spend that is really an N-spend, and the same shape yields multi-redeemed gift cards and over-limit transfers.\n\nThe pattern generalizes to any 'once' or 'up to N' rule \u2014 applying a single-use discount repeatedly, exceeding a per-account purchase cap, multi-voting, or claiming a one-per-user reward again and again \u2014 so every business invariant from the previous week that is enforced by a check-then-update is race-exploitable. The attack is timing rather than payload: the identical, fully legitimate request is sent many times simultaneously, relying on the window, with nothing malformed to detect; it is the emergent-harm idea of business logic with concurrency as the lever.\n\nThe defense is to enforce the limit atomically at the data layer \u2014 a conditional update that decrements only if the invariant still holds, row locking, or a uniqueness constraint on one-time actions \u2014 so that of N concurrent requests, only those the limit actually permits succeed.",w24l3:"Because a race is a timing window, exploiting one means landing multiple requests inside it, which in practice involves sending requests as simultaneously as possible \u2014 parallel connections and techniques that release many requests at the same instant \u2014 so they all reach the server before the first one's update commits. The window can sometimes be widened: if part of the operation is slow, such as an external call or a large computation, the gap between check and commit grows and the race becomes far easier to win, so attackers look for anything that delays the commit.\n\nRecognition is the real skill, as it was with business logic. Race-prone operations have a tell \u2014 a check-then-act on shared state, especially anything described as 'once', 'first', 'up to', 'claim', 'redeem', or 'apply', or a multi-step flow where state is read in one request and changed in another. It also connects back to the two-systems pattern that recurs throughout the course: a race is two or more executions disagreeing about the current state because they overlap in time, like front-end versus back-end in request smuggling or sanitizer versus browser in mutation XSS, except that the disagreeing parties are concurrent copies of the same code.\n\nThe defensive consequence is that timing windows cannot be exhaustively tested, so they must be designed out: assume any check-then-act on shared state will be raced and make it atomic, treating one-time and limited operations as requiring a database-level guarantee rather than application-level sequencing.",w24l4:"Races are not out-tested but designed out, and the tool is atomicity: making the check and the act a single indivisible operation. A database transaction with appropriate row locking, or an atomic conditional update that decrements only where the invariant still holds, ensures concurrent requests serialize correctly instead of all passing a stale check. For one-time actions a uniqueness constraint or an idempotency key is decisive \u2014 if redeeming coupon X for user Y must happen once, a unique index on the pair makes the second concurrent attempt fail at the data layer regardless of timing \u2014 because only the database sees all the requests at once. The anti-pattern to retire is application-level sequencing, checking in code and then updating in code, which assumes requests are processed one at a time; under concurrency they are not, so the guarantee must live where the contention is resolved.\n\nStepping back to the whole semester, files (traversal, inclusion, upload), identity (authentication, authorization, JWT, SSO), and logic (business logic, races) were each the server trusting the client to stay within a boundary it never actually enforced \u2014 the filesystem, its own permissions, the intended workflow, and finally the assumption of sequential time. The unifying defender principle across all of App and Access is therefore to name the invariant and enforce it on the server at the layer that actually owns it: confine the path, authorize the subject-action-object, validate the workflow state, and make limited actions atomic.\n\nThis closes Semester 3, leaving a single discipline carried into the rest of the course \u2014 boundaries are only real where the server enforces them.",w25l1:"XML is not merely a data format but a format with features, and the dangerous one is the entity. A Document Type Definition can declare entities \u2014 shorthand that the parser expands \u2014 and an external entity declares that the shorthand's value should be fetched from a URL or file path. An XML document can therefore instruct the parser to define an entity as the contents of file:///etc/passwd, and a parser that resolves external entities will read that file and substitute it wherever the entity is referenced. The document controls what the parser fetches.\n\nThat is XML External Entity injection in a sentence: untrusted XML is parsed by a parser that honors external entities, so the attacker's document makes the server read files or make requests. It is injection in which the interpreter is the XML parser and the dangerous capability is 'fetch this resource.' Historically many parsers resolved external entities by default, which is why XXE has been so widespread \u2014 the vulnerability was the out-of-the-box configuration, present anywhere an application parses XML it did not generate.\n\nThe defensive point is that the feature, not the data, is the problem: disabling DTD processing and external entity resolution makes a parser that cannot fetch external resources, and such a parser cannot be made to leak files or reach internal services regardless of the document.",w25l2:"A working XXE offers two immediate payoffs. The first is file disclosure: an external entity pointing at file:///etc/passwd \u2014 or, more valuably, the application's configuration, source, or keys \u2014 placed where the response echoes data causes the parser to read the file and return its contents. The second is reach: an external entity can point at an http:// URL, so the parser makes a request to it, and the server then fetches whatever the attacker names \u2014 an internal admin service, a database dashboard, a cloud metadata endpoint \u2014 from its trusted network position. That is server-side request forgery, the subject of the next week.\n\nThe shift in power is the essential point: XXE turns the server into the attacker's file reader and HTTP client, granting them its filesystem access and its network location and thus files and hosts they could never reach directly. Even simple XXE escalates readily \u2014 reading credentials from a configuration file and then using them, or hitting a cloud metadata service to steal instance credentials \u2014 so file read combined with internal reach is frequently a path to far more than disclosure.\n\nThe defense is unchanged: disable external entities and DTDs so that neither file nor URL fetch is possible, with the defense-in-depth measure that the XML-parsing service should not have broad filesystem access or unrestricted outbound network, limiting the blast radius if a parser is misconfigured.",w25l3:"Frequently the parsed XML's value never appears in the response: the parser resolves the entity but nothing is echoed. Blind XXE extracts data anyway, out-of-band, by making the parser send the data to a server the attacker controls rather than waiting for reflection. The technique uses parameter entities \u2014 entities usable inside the DTD \u2014 together with an external DTD hosted by the attacker: that DTD defines an entity which reads the target file, then defines another that builds a URL containing the file's contents and forces the parser to fetch it, so the file's bytes ride out in the request to the attacker's server. Error-based out-of-band exfiltration is the fallback, crafting the entities so the file's contents are placed where they trigger a parser error whose message, returned to the attacker, leaks the data.\n\nThis is the same idea as blind SQL injection: with no direct output, the attacker builds a side channel \u2014 an outbound request or an error message \u2014 that carries the secret, so blindness slows the attacker without saving the data. Blind and out-of-band XXE therefore prove that 'we do not show the parsed result' is not a defense, because the parser's ability to fetch is the bug. Disabling external entities and DTDs, with blocking the parsing host's outbound network as a depth measure, leaves no channel to build.",w25l4:"The fix for XXE is simple and total: configure the XML parser to disable DTD processing and external entity resolution, since a parser that will not fetch cannot be made to leak files or reach internal hosts regardless of the document \u2014 one setting, done correctly, closes the whole class. The genuine difficulty is noticing that XML is being parsed at all, because XXE hides far beyond obvious XML APIs. Office documents such as DOCX and XLSX, and SVG images, are zipped or embedded XML, so a file-upload feature that processes them is parsing XML, and the uploads of Week 18 meet XXE here. It also lurks in SOAP web services, in SAML assertions (attacked in Week 22, and themselves XML), in RSS and Atom feeds, and in configuration files \u2014 any of which, parsed by a vulnerable parser, is an XXE sink.\n\nThe sneakiest case is a JSON endpoint that also accepts XML: if the API parses the body according to its Content-Type, switching the Content-Type to application/xml with an XXE payload turns an endpoint that 'only takes JSON' into an XML parser, the content-type confusion of the CSRF week pointed at XXE.\n\nThe defensive conclusion is to harden every XML parser in the stack \u2014 libraries, document processors, SOAP and SAML handlers \u2014 to reject DTDs and external entities, and to audit upload and feed features for hidden XML parsing. Recognizing the sink is most of the battle; the fix itself is a single configuration.",w26l1:"Server-side request forgery, already glimpsed through XXE, is making the server fetch a URL the attacker controls. Any feature that accepts a URL or hostname and has the server retrieve it is a candidate \u2014 webhooks, link previews, PDF and image fetchers, import-from-URL, SSO discovery. The power lies entirely in the server's network position: the attacker's browser cannot reach the server's localhost, its internal network, or the cloud metadata endpoint, but the server can, and SSRF makes it do so on the attacker's behalf, borrowing its place inside the perimeter.\n\nThe interesting targets are therefore not on the public internet but inside it: http://localhost/admin for services bound to loopback on the assumption they are private, internal applications on private ranges, and the cloud metadata service. The firewall that protects these from the outside does not apply to a request originating from the server itself. Even blind SSRF, where no response is returned, is useful, since timing and error differences confirm internal hosts and open ports (port scanning from inside) and state-changing internal endpoints can still be hit; reflected SSRF additionally returns the fetched content.\n\nThe defense is to refuse to let user input choose the fetch target: validate against an allowlist of permitted hosts, resolve and re-check the final IP against internal ranges, disable unneeded URL schemes, and isolate the fetching service so that being 'inside' is not valuable.",w26l2:"The crown-jewel SSRF target is the cloud metadata service. On many cloud platforms an instance reads its own configuration, including temporary credentials, from a link-local address such as 169.254.169.254, and because the service trusts any request from the instance, SSRF that reaches it returns the instance's cloud credentials. Those credentials usually carry the instance's role and are therefore powerful, so SSRF-to-metadata escalates directly to cloud-account compromise \u2014 reading storage buckets, creating resources, and pivoting across the environment \u2014 turning a single link-preview bug into a breach of the entire account.\n\nInternal services are the other prize, since many run unauthenticated on the assumption that only trusted callers can reach them; SSRF reaches them from inside, and 'no authentication because it is internal' becomes 'no authentication at all.' SSRF can also reach non-HTTP services and become remote code execution: with schemes such as gopher:// an attacker crafts arbitrary bytes to speak another protocol, for example commanding an exposed Redis to write a file or job, chaining SSRF into execution.\n\nThe defenses are to harden the metadata service by requiring the modern token-based version and denying by default, to authenticate internal services regardless of network location so that reaching them is insufficient, to restrict outbound schemes to http and https, and to segment the fetching service so it cannot reach metadata or sensitive internal hosts.",w26l3:"The instinct to blocklist SSRF targets \u2014 refusing 'localhost', '127.0.0.1', or the metadata IP \u2014 fails in the way every blocklist fails, because there are too many ways to write the same destination and too many layers that can redirect after the check. Address encodings are the first bypass: 127.0.0.1 is equally 2130706433 in decimal, 0x7f000001 in hex, 017700000001 in octal, and has IPv6 forms, and the metadata IP has equivalents too, so a string blocklist sees a different string while the network stack resolves the same host.\n\nDNS and redirects add indirection. A hostname the attacker controls can resolve to an internal IP, so the check sees a benign domain while the fetch hits an internal address, and an allowed URL can return an HTTP redirect to an internal target that the fetcher follows after the check has passed. In each case the validator and the actual fetch disagree about the destination \u2014 the recurring two-systems gap, in which the validator inspects one representation or moment and the network stack acts on another. A blocklist of strings can never enumerate every encoding, name, and redirect that resolves to 'inside.'\n\nThe defense is to allowlist permitted destinations rather than blocklist bad ones, to resolve the hostname and validate the final IP against denied ranges (loopback, private, link-local) immediately before fetching, to disable redirects or re-validate each hop, and to pin the connection to the validated IP so DNS cannot change underneath the check.",w26l4:"DNS rebinding defeats even an IP-validating SSRF filter, and it is a race condition on name resolution. The attacker's domain first resolves to a harmless public IP, which the validator checks and approves, and then, with a very short DNS time-to-live, re-resolves to an internal IP for the actual fetch, so time-of-check used one IP while time-of-use used another. The flaw is that resolving the hostname to validate and then resolving again to connect leaves a gap in which the attacker changes the answer, and the validator and the connector disagree about what the name points to \u2014 the two-moments gap, now on DNS.\n\nThe fix closes that gap: resolve the hostname exactly once, validate that IP, and pin the connection to it, so the fetch uses the very IP that was checked and there is nothing left to rebind. This sits inside the full SSRF architecture that synthesizes the week \u2014 allowlist destinations, resolve-once-and-pin, deny loopback, private, and link-local on the resolved IP, restrict schemes to http and https, control redirects, harden the metadata service to its token-based form, and authenticate internal services so that reaching them is not enough.\n\nThe defensive theme, shared with XXE, is that both are the server fetching attacker-chosen resources from its trusted position, so the cure is never to let untrusted input determine what the server fetches and never to treat 'inside the perimeter' as equivalent to 'trusted.' The next week turns to HTTP request smuggling, where the disagreement between components is about request boundaries rather than destinations.",w27l1:"Week 1 previewed it; this lesson makes it concrete. When a front-end proxy and a back-end server disagree about where one HTTP request ends and the next begins, an attacker can smuggle a request, and the disagreement is about length. HTTP/1.1 offers two ways to state a body's length: Content-Length, which says the body is N bytes, and Transfer-Encoding: chunked, which says the body is a series of chunks ending with a zero-size chunk. If a single request contains both, each server must decide which to honor, and if they choose differently they parse different boundaries.\n\nThat boundary disagreement is the entire bug. Because connections between the front-end and back-end are typically reused for many users' requests, bytes one server treats as leftover are prepended to the next request the other server reads, usually the next user's, so the attacker's smuggled prefix rides on someone else's connection. It is the two-parsers-disagree pattern in its purest form, the same shape as SOP bypass (origin parsers), mutation XSS (sanitizer versus browser), and SSRF filter evasion (validator versus network stack), with the proxy and the origin as the disagreeing parsers.\n\nThe defensive principle is that ambiguity is the enemy: a request specifying both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding should be rejected rather than interpreted, the whole chain should agree on framing, and (as the next lesson shows) the named variants arise from which server trusts which header.",w27l2:"The classic desyncs are named by which length header each server honors, front-end first. CL.TE means the front-end uses Content-Length while the back-end uses Transfer-Encoding; TE.CL is the reverse; and TE.TE describes the case where both support Transfer-Encoding but one is tricked into ignoring it through an obfuscated header. In CL.TE, the attacker sends a request whose Content-Length covers the whole body, so the front-end forwards all of it, while its chunked encoding tells the back-end the request ended earlier, leaving the trailing bytes as the start of what the back-end reads as the next request. TE.CL is the mirror: the front-end honors the chunked encoding and forwards a request it considers complete, while the back-end, using Content-Length, stops short and treats the remainder as a new request \u2014 the same outcome through the opposite disagreement.\n\nThe craft lies in making the two servers compute different lengths from the same bytes, frequently by obfuscating one header so that a server which should not ignore Transfer-Encoding does \u2014 via a space, a duplicate, or unusual casing \u2014 which is parser-quirk exploitation, the same instinct as encoding bypasses and double-extension uploads.\n\nDefensively, the variants are not memorized to defend against but eliminated: normalize and validate framing at the front-end, reject requests containing both headers or malformed Transfer-Encoding, and prefer a back-end that rejects ambiguity rather than guessing.",w27l3:"A smuggled prefix is not the attack but a primitive: it sits at the front of a reused connection so that the next request to arrive, typically a real user's, is interpreted as a continuation of the attacker's smuggled bytes. Several serious impacts follow. Capturing a victim's request is the most severe \u2014 a prefix crafted so the back-end treats the victim's incoming request, with its cookies and credentials, as the body of an attacker request to an endpoint that stores or reflects it, handing the victim's session to the attacker. Cache poisoning operates at scale: a smuggled request whose response is cached against a popular URL causes every subsequent user to be served attacker-controlled content, so one desynced request poisons everyone hitting that cache key.\n\nBypassing front-end controls is structural, because security checks such as authentication, WAF rules, and path restrictions often live on the front-end, while a smuggled request reaches the back-end without passing through them, so the back-end processes a request the front-end never saw or validated; CVE-2019-20372 is a real NGINX example.\n\nThe defensive observation is that all of these impacts flow from the boundary disagreement, so they close with the same fix \u2014 eliminate the framing ambiguity and stop reusing back-end connections across users, leaving no shared connection for a prefix to poison and no disagreement to create one.",w27l4:"Defending request smuggling is not a matter of enumerating variants but of removing the disagreement: reject any request that is ambiguous about its own length (both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding, or malformed chunked encoding) and normalize framing so the front-end and back-end cannot interpret the same bytes differently. HTTP/2 end-to-end is the structural fix and closes the loop with Week 1, because HTTP/2's binary framing states length unambiguously and removes the Content-Length-versus-Transfer-Encoding choice; the danger reappeared, in Week 1, only at the HTTP/2-to-HTTP/1.1 downgrade, so HTTP/2 should be kept all the way to the back-end where possible. Connection reuse is the other lever: a back-end that uses a fresh connection per client request offers no shared stream for a smuggled prefix to poison, and many real-world fixes were exactly that.\n\nStepping back, smuggling is the purest example of a pattern recurring throughout the course \u2014 two components parsing the same bytes by different rules. SOP bypass (two origin parsers), mutation XSS (sanitizer versus browser), double-encoding (filter depth versus sink), DNS rebinding (check versus connect), and smuggling (proxy versus origin) are one idea: wherever two parsers meet, they must be made to agree or to reject what they cannot.\n\nThe semester's theme so far unites XXE, SSRF, and smuggling as the server and its infrastructure acting on attacker-shaped data and requests at a boundary it did not fully control. The next week shifts from requests to data with insecure deserialization, where rebuilding an object becomes running code.",w28l1:"Serialization turns a program's in-memory objects into a byte stream so they can be stored in a session, cache, cookie, or message, and deserialization rebuilds the objects from those bytes. The danger is simple: if the bytes are attacker-controlled, the attacker controls which objects are reconstructed and what their properties are. This is a profound amount of control, because unlike a string that is later parsed, a serialized blob directly specifies typed objects and their internal state, so deserializing untrusted data amounts to letting an attacker instantiate arbitrary objects in the process with attacker-chosen fields.\n\nThe format is the tell \u2014 PHP's serialize() output, Java's serialized stream, Python pickle, .NET's BinaryFormatter and ViewState \u2014 and wherever such a blob arrives from the client (a cookie, a hidden field, a token, an upload, a message queue) and is deserialized, the attacker can shape the objects. On its own, instructing the program to create an object with certain fields might seem harmless; it becomes dangerous because reconstruction is not passive \u2014 the language runs code during and after it (the next lesson's magic methods) and the attacker chooses which objects' code runs, so data turns into behavior.\n\nThe defensive rule is blunt: do not deserialize untrusted data with a code-capable deserializer. Prefer simple data-only formats such as JSON or protobuf parsed into known types, and if serialized objects must be accepted, sign them for integrity so that only the server's own bytes are ever deserialized.",w28l2:"Deserialization is dangerous because reconstruction triggers code automatically: many languages invoke lifecycle or magic methods on objects as they are built or destroyed \u2014 PHP's __wakeup() and __destruct() among them \u2014 without the application asking, so the attacker, by choosing which objects exist, chooses whose auto-invoked methods fire. A gadget is an existing class already in the codebase or its libraries whose magic method performs an action useful to the attacker, such as writing a file, making a request, running a command, or deleting data; the attacker does not write code but selects a class whose code already does what they want and gets it instantiated.\n\nA single gadget is rarely sufficient, so attackers chain them in a property-oriented programming (POP) chain, wiring gadgets together so that one magic method calls a method on a property the attacker set, which is itself another gadget, and so on, composing existing code fragments into an exploit \u2014 programming with the application's own classes. The profound aspect is that no new code is injected: every method that runs already existed and was intended to be safe in normal use, and the attacker supplied only data \u2014 which objects and which fields \u2014 with reconstruction doing the rest, the most literal instance of 'data becomes code' in the course.\n\nDefensively, gadgets cannot easily be removed because they are legitimate classes, so the prevention is to stop attacker-controlled deserialization in the first place; magic methods executing on reconstruction is by design, and the bug is letting an attacker choose what gets reconstructed.",w28l3:"PHP object injection is the concrete, classic case. PHP's unserialize() rebuilds objects from a string, so if that string is attacker-controlled \u2014 a cookie, a parameter, a cache entry \u2014 the attacker instantiates arbitrary classes with arbitrary properties. PHP then runs magic methods on those objects automatically: __wakeup() immediately after unserialize, __destruct() when the object is cleaned up, and __toString() if it is treated as a string, so an attacker-instantiated object whose magic method does something dangerous fires without the application calling anything.\n\nThe exploit is finding the gadget: scanning the application and its frameworks for a class whose __destruct or __wakeup, or a method they reach, performs a file write, an include, a deletion, or a command, then serializing that class with the properties that aim it at the target, a process made systematic by tools and catalogs of known framework gadget chains. It frequently reaches remote code execution, because a gadget that writes a file into the webroot (Week 18), includes a path (Week 17), or invokes a callable becomes execution, all from a single attacker-controlled serialized string handed to unserialize().\n\nThe defense is to never call unserialize() on untrusted input: use json_decode() for data, restrict the allowed classes if unserialize is unavoidable, and integrity-protect any serialized data that must round-trip through the client so it cannot be tampered with.",w28l4:"Insecure deserialization is unusually prone to remote code execution because of what it attacks: the language's own object machinery. Other injections reach an interpreter for a specific language, such as SQL or the shell, whereas deserialization hands the attacker the runtime itself \u2014 arbitrary objects, their methods, and the gadget classes already present \u2014 so code execution is frequently the default outcome rather than the ceiling. It is the 'data becomes code' idea at its most literal: SQL injection mixed data into a query and SSTI mixed data into a template, but here the data is itself a description of objects and behavior reconstructed directly into the running program, with no language boundary to break because the serialized format is already the program's internal representation.\n\nRecognition is the practical skill, because the sink is often invisible. Learning the serialized formats by sight \u2014 PHP's s:/a:/O: structure, a Java stream that base64-encodes starting with 'rO0', .NET ViewState, Python pickle opcodes \u2014 and looking for them in cookies, hidden fields, tokens, caches, and message queues finds the round-trips where objects cross the client. The cross-language truth, developed next week, is that every major platform has this bug \u2014 PHP, Java, .NET, Python \u2014 because each auto-runs code during reconstruction and ships classes that form gadgets; the format differs while the bug and the cure do not.\n\nThe defensive rule is to not deserialize untrusted data with a code-capable deserializer, using data-only formats parsed into known types and, where serialized objects must cross a trust boundary, signing them for integrity and allowlisting permitted classes. This sets up the next week's tour across .NET, Python, and Java.",w29l1:".NET deserialization repeats the pattern with its own machinery. The notorious sink is BinaryFormatter, along with relatives such as LosFormatter and NetDataContractSerializer: deserializing attacker-controlled data with them instantiates arbitrary types and triggers known gadget chains to remote code execution, and Microsoft itself now warns that BinaryFormatter is dangerous and deprecated. The everyday .NET case is ViewState, the serialized page state ASP.NET round-trips through a hidden form field; if it is not integrity-protected, an attacker tampers with the serialized object graph and deserialization yields RCE.\n\nThe protection is the MAC, a keyed integrity check: ViewState signed with a secret key cannot be tampered with, so deserialization only ever sees the server's own object graph. The bug is ViewState with the MAC disabled or, worse, a leaked, guessable, or default machine key, which lets the attacker forge valid signed ViewState. .NET deserialization therefore has two failure modes \u2014 a code-capable formatter on untrusted input, and integrity that is missing or defeated \u2014 and tools like ysoserial.net generate the gadget payloads once either door is open.\n\nDefensively, BinaryFormatter and similar should not be used on untrusted data (a data-only serializer with type restrictions is preferred), the ViewState MAC should be kept on with a strong, secret, per-application machine key, and a leaked machine key should be treated as a full compromise and rotated.",w29l2:"Python's pickle is the starkest case, because pickle is not a data format but a mini-program format: the pickle stream contains opcodes, one of which invokes a callable, so unpickling attacker data can run arbitrary code by design rather than by gadget hunting. The mechanism is the __reduce__ method, by which a class defines how it is pickled by returning a callable and its arguments that unpickling then calls; an attacker crafts a pickle whose __reduce__ returns os.system with their command, and pickle.loads() runs it, with no chain and no magic-method search required.\n\nThe rule for Python is therefore absolute and well known: never unpickle data that is not fully trusted, because there is no safe subset for untrusted input \u2014 pickle's purpose includes reconstructing arbitrary objects, which includes running code. pickle also hides in convenient places such as caches, machine-learning model files, Python-to-Python message queues, and some session backends, so loading a .pkl or restoring from cache on attacker-influenced data is direct RCE, which makes untrusted model and artifact files a real supply-chain risk.\n\nDefensively, untrusted data should use a data-only format \u2014 JSON for simple data or a safe schema-based serializer \u2014 and pickle should be reserved strictly for data that never crosses a trust boundary; where pickled data must be accepted, it should be signed and verified before loading.",w29l3:"Java deserialization made the bug famous. Any class implementing Serializable can be deserialized, and readObject() and related callbacks run during reconstruction, so, as in PHP and .NET, an attacker who controls the serialized stream chooses objects whose callbacks execute. Java's particular twist is the gadget library problem: widely-used libraries on the classpath, the classic being Apache Commons Collections, contain gadget chains powerful enough to reach RCE, and because these libraries are everywhere a Java application deserializing untrusted data is frequently exploitable with off-the-shelf chains.\n\nysoserial is the well-known tool that generates ready-made gadget-chain payloads for many common libraries, so exploitation often reduces to identifying which library is on the classpath rather than hand-crafting a chain \u2014 the ubiquity of the libraries is the vulnerability's reach. Detection uses a harmless probe: the URLDNS gadget makes deserialization trigger a DNS lookup to an attacker domain, with no code execution, just a DNS hit confirming that the endpoint deserializes attacker input \u2014 the same out-of-band confirmation idea as blind XXE and blind SQL injection.\n\nDefensively, untrusted Java objects should not be deserialized; where unavoidable, look-ahead deserialization that allowlists permitted classes before instantiation should be used, libraries kept patched, and data-only formats preferred. The classpath that cannot be fully controlled is exactly why allowlisting at deserialization matters.",w29l4:"Four runtimes exhibit one bug: PHP with unserialize and magic methods, .NET with BinaryFormatter and ViewState, Python with pickle and __reduce__, and Java with readObject and gadget libraries all auto-run code while reconstructing objects and all ship classes that form gadgets, so deserializing untrusted data is RCE-prone everywhere. The format and the gadgets differ while the root cause is identical, which means the cure is identical and language-independent: do not deserialize untrusted data with a code-capable deserializer, and recognize that the single most effective control is choosing the right format for the trust boundary rather than patching gadgets that can never be fully enumerated.\n\nThe defenses form a hierarchy. First, use a data-only format such as JSON or protobuf parsed into known types for anything crossing a trust boundary, since data formats do not reconstruct arbitrary objects or run code. Second, if serialized objects must be accepted, allowlist permitted classes at deserialization (look-ahead) and keep dependencies patched. Third, integrity-protect serialized blobs that round-trip by signing them, so that only the server's own bytes are ever deserialized.\n\nThis closes the data-and-services theme so far, in which XXE, SSRF, request smuggling, and deserialization are all the server acting on attacker-shaped input it reconstructs or relays \u2014 parsing, fetching, framing, and rebuilding \u2014 each fixed by not trusting the structure of attacker input: disable the dangerous capability, validate at the boundary, and prefer formats that cannot carry code. The remaining weeks move from these parsing bugs to the service surface \u2014 SOAP, REST, GraphQL, and serverless \u2014 where the question becomes what the API exposes and trusts.",w3l1:"A browser is best understood as a runtime that executes untrusted code from many mutually distrusting parties simultaneously, all while holding the user's cookies, sessions, and saved credentials. Its central security responsibility is therefore isolation: keeping one party's code from observing or acting on another's data.\n\nThe principal that isolation is built around is the origin \u2014 the triple of scheme, host, and port. https://app.com, http://app.com, and https://app.com:8443 are three distinct origins, each with its own walled-off storage and DOM access. Neither the registrable domain nor the individual page is the boundary; the origin is. Modern browsers reinforce this with a multi-process model, placing different sites in different operating-system processes so that a memory-safety bug or a speculative-execution side channel in one cannot trivially reach another.\n\nTwo engines do the dangerous work. The rendering engine parses HTML and CSS, and the JavaScript engine executes script \u2014 both operating on bytes delivered straight from the network and running inside the user's authenticated session. This is why parser ambiguities and script sinks are treated as security defects rather than cosmetic issues: they are the points where attacker-controlled input becomes execution.\n\nEvery control in the rest of this week answers one question: may code from origin A observe or act on origin B's data? Same-origin enforcement, content policies, and cookie attributes are all rules layered on that question, and the bugs are the exceptions to those rules.",w3l2:"The Same-Origin Policy (SOP) is the browser's default-deny rule for cross-origin reads: script running in one origin may not read the responses of another. It is the floor on which every other browser control is built, raising or lowering what is permitted.\n\nThe crucial property is that SOP is asymmetric. It restricts reading far more than sending. The browser will happily issue many cross-origin requests \u2014 form submissions, image and script loads \u2014 and it will attach the user's cookies to them; what it withholds is the ability for script to read the response. 'Can send, cannot read' is not a quirk but the defining shape of the policy, and it directly explains two later subjects: cross-site request forgery exploits the ability to send a state-changing request without needing to read anything, and Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) exists precisely to grant a controlled, server-opted-in exception to the read restriction.\n\nThe assumption worth probing as an attacker is that SOP protects a given piece of data at all. It protects script-readable responses, not every interaction. Cross-origin embeds run, cross-origin posts land, and side channels \u2014 request timing, response size, the firing of load or error events \u2014 can leak the very bits SOP was meant to conceal.\n\nFor a defender, the discipline is to treat SOP as a floor rather than a wall: enumerate what it does not block, and configure CORS as a strict allowlist of known origins rather than reflecting whatever Origin header arrives (especially when credentials are allowed).",w3l3:"Content Security Policy (CSP) is a response header that constrains which sources of script, style, and other resources the browser will load and execute for a page. It is essential to frame it correctly: CSP does not fix an injection vulnerability, it limits what an injected payload can accomplish once present. It is defense-in-depth layered on top of the real fix, which is correct output encoding.\n\nThe value is in shrinking the blast radius. With a strict policy, an attacker who manages to inject markup may still find their inline script refused execution and their exfiltration endpoint refused loading. The two failure modes that hollow out real-world policies are predictable: 'unsafe-inline', which re-permits exactly the inline script the policy was meant to stop, and over-broad source allowlists, where a single allowlisted CDN that happens to host a JSONP endpoint or an outdated library becomes the attacker's route back to script execution.\n\nA sound policy avoids unsafe-inline and unsafe-eval and instead authorizes inline script by nonce or hash: each legitimate script carries a per-response random nonce (or matches a known hash), and injected script, unable to supply it, does not run. The techniques for bypassing weak policies are developed in Semester 2; the goal here is to recognize what a strong policy looks like.\n\nThe defender's takeaway: pair CSP with output encoding, prefer nonces or hashes over unsafe-inline, and remember that every allowlisted origin is code you have chosen to trust.",w3l4:"After the broad policies come the per-feature controls, and for sessions the most important live on the cookie itself. A cookie is attached automatically to matching requests, so its security is largely a function of its attributes. Secure restricts it to HTTPS, preventing plaintext leakage. HttpOnly removes it from the JavaScript-readable surface, so an injected script cannot read the session out of document.cookie. SameSite governs whether the cookie accompanies cross-site requests, which is the primary lever against cross-site request forgery. A session cookie missing HttpOnly or Secure is a finding on sight.\n\nThree more controls tighten the surrounding walls. The iframe sandbox attribute strips an embedded frame to zero capability by default \u2014 no script, no forms, no top-level navigation \u2014 and re-grants individual capabilities only as explicit tokens, which is least privilege for untrusted embeds. Subresource Integrity (SRI) pins a cryptographic hash to a script or stylesheet loaded from a third party, so a compromised CDN serving altered bytes is rejected by the browser. HSTS instructs the browser to use HTTPS for the domain for a set duration, closing the brief downgrade-to-http window an attacker would otherwise use to strip TLS.\n\nNone of these are exotic, and that is the point: they are the knobs that decide whether the isolation described all week is actually enforced. Set cookie flags correctly, sandbox untrusted iframes, pin third-party code with SRI, and enable HSTS \u2014 each closes a specific, well-documented attack path, and their absence is what turns a theoretical boundary into a real one for the attacker.",w30l1:"A web API \u2014 whether SOAP, REST, or GraphQL \u2014 is the application's functionality exposed directly, without the user interface in front of it. Everything the UI implicitly did for security, such as only showing fields a user is allowed to see, only offering actions a user is allowed to take, and not advertising endpoints, is gone, and the API must enforce all of it explicitly, which it often does not. Two architectural styles frame the topic: RPC-style services (SOAP, JSON-RPC) expose methods called by name, while REST exposes resources acted on with HTTP verbs; the style differs but the security question is identical \u2014 what does the endpoint expose, and what does it actually check?\n\nThe recurring mistake is assuming the client is the official one. Developers picture their own front-end calling the API politely, sending only the intended fields and hitting only the intended endpoints, and build the API to trust that, while an attacker uses the API directly and ignores every UI-imposed limit. The API attack surface is therefore wider than the app's: undocumented methods and parameters, full objects where the UI showed a summary, and operations the UI never exposed but the API still serves \u2014 the same don't-trust-the-client theme with no UI to hide behind.\n\nDefensively, the API must be treated as the only security boundary: it must authenticate, authorize per object and per field, validate input, and expose only what is intended, regardless of what the UI does. The front-end is a convenience, never a control.",w30l2:"SOAP is RPC over XML and is self-describing: a WSDL document lists every operation, its parameters, and its types, which is convenient for clients and a map for attackers enumerating methods the UI never calls, including administrative or debug operations left exposed. Invoking hidden methods is the classic SOAP attack \u2014 the WSDL or guessed operation names reveal operations the front-end does not use, such as createAdmin, exportData, or an internal utility, and if the service authorizes by the assumption that the UI would not call them rather than by an actual check, calling them directly succeeds.\n\nBecause SOAP is XML, it inherits the XXE topic: a SOAP endpoint parsing the request body is an XXE sink unless external entities are disabled, and SOAP message fields concatenated into queries or commands are injection sinks like any other input \u2014 the envelope is simply more attacker-controlled XML. The theme holds that SOAP services often trust callers to use the documented operations in the intended way, so the bugs are unexposed-but-reachable methods and unvalidated message contents, the API surface beyond the UI.\n\nDefensively, every operation should be authorized explicitly rather than relying on it being unadvertised, the WSDL and internal operations should be restricted or removed in production, the XML parser should be hardened against XXE, and every field of the SOAP message should be validated as untrusted input.",w30l3:"REST APIs concentrate three signature flaws, all flowing from the absence of the UI. The first is excessive data exposure: the API returns the full object \u2014 every field of a user record \u2014 and trusts the client to display only some, so an attacker reading the raw response sees fields such as emails, roles, internal flags, or password hashes that the UI hid. The second is broken object-level authorization (BOLA), which is IDOR for APIs and the top API risk: a request such as GET /api/orders/123 returns order 123, and changing the identifier to 124 returns another user's order when there is no per-object ownership check; APIs make this pervasive because every object has a clean, enumerable endpoint.\n\nThe third is mass assignment, or auto-binding: the API binds the incoming JSON straight onto the data model, so fields the form never showed \u2014 role, isAdmin, accountBalance, isVerified \u2014 can be set simply by including them in the request body. All three share one root: the API trusted the client to send and receive only the intended fields and objects, because that is what the official UI does, so reading extra (exposure), addressing others' objects (BOLA), and writing extra (mass assignment) all exploit the absent UI restraint.\n\nDefensively, the API should return only the fields the caller may see through explicit response shaping rather than client-side filtering, enforce per-object ownership on every resource access to stop BOLA, and bind only an allowlist of permitted input fields to the model to block mass assignment. The contract is defined server-side and never inherited from the UI's behavior.",w30l4:"The web-services topic distilled is that an API is a contract, and security is enforcing that contract server-side regardless of which client calls it: authentication of who is calling, per-object and per-field authorization of what they may see and do, input validation, and exposing only intended operations are the API's responsibility on every request, not the UI's. API authentication is its own surface \u2014 tokens and keys must be validated on every endpoint rather than only the obviously authenticated pages, scoped to least privilege, and kept out of URLs and logs, since an unauthenticated 'internal' API endpoint is exactly the SSRF and BOLA jackpot of earlier weeks. Versioning and documentation are attack surface too: old API versions such as a /v1 left beside /v2 often lack newer fixes, and a public schema, WSDL, or spec enumerates everything, so old versions should be retired and undocumented endpoints should never be assumed hidden, because reconnaissance finds them.\n\nThis is the access-control topic of the access-control semester re-applied without a UI: BOLA is IDOR, mass assignment is parameter tampering, and excessive exposure is over-trusting the client to filter \u2014 the API simply removes the front-end that was accidentally masking the missing checks. Defensively, the contract is defined and enforced centrally: authenticate every request, authorize per object and per field, allowlist inputs and shape outputs, and version deliberately. The next topic, GraphQL, gives the client even more power to ask for exactly what it wants, sharpening every one of these questions.",w31l1:"GraphQL inverts the API model: instead of many fixed endpoints each returning a fixed shape, there is one endpoint and the client sends a query describing exactly which fields it wants and how deeply nested, so power moves to the client \u2014 which is the whole security story for the topic. The schema is strongly typed and, by default, introspectable: a special query (__schema) returns the entire API, including every type, field, argument, and mutation, which is SOAP's self-describing WSDL problem amplified, handing an attacker a complete, machine-readable map that includes fields and operations the UI never uses.\n\nBecause one endpoint serves everything, the old 'protect this URL' model breaks: there is no per-endpoint authorization to attach, so access control must live deeper, at the field and object level, or it is not enforced at all. The client's query freedom also allows traversing relationships \u2014 from a user to their orders to each order's payment to the payer's profile \u2014 following edges the UI would never chain, and if any resolver along that path does not check authorization the nesting reaches data the caller should not see.\n\nDefensively, introspection should be disabled in production \u2014 not relied on for secrecy, but not volunteered either \u2014 and GraphQL's single typed endpoint should be understood to relocate every classic API concern (authorization, data exposure, rate limiting) from the URL to the resolver and the query shape.",w31l2:"In GraphQL, every field is backed by a resolver, a function that fetches that field's value, so authorization must live in those resolvers because there is no per-endpoint check to rely on; a resolver that returns data without checking who is asking exposes the field to anyone who can name it. BOLA and broken authorization therefore reappear per field: a query for user(id: 124){ email } leaks another user's email if the email resolver or the user lookup does not verify ownership \u2014 the same IDOR/BOLA bug as REST, now at field granularity across a large typed surface.\n\nNested traversal makes this worse, because even when a top-level field is protected, a path through a permitted object to a sensitive nested field can slip past when developers secure the obvious entry points and forget the resolvers reachable only by traversal; every resolver on every path is a boundary, not just the root. Mutations are the write side: a mutation the UI never exposes, such as deleteUser or setRole, remains in the schema and callable, and as with SOAP's hidden methods, 'the front-end does not offer it' is not authorization \u2014 the mutation resolver must check.\n\nDefensively, authorization should be enforced in every resolver (or a centralized layer the resolvers call), per object and per field, for queries and mutations alike, treating the schema as fully reachable and assuming the attacker will query every field and traverse every edge.",w31l3:"The client's query freedom is also a denial-of-service surface: because the caller controls depth and breadth, a single small request can demand enormous work through deeply nested queries, recursive relationships, and wide field selections that each fan out into many database calls. The classic case is a deeply nested recursive query \u2014 if posts have authors who have posts who have authors, a query can nest that relationship dozens of levels deep and the server resolves an exponential amount of data from one cheap-looking request, an amplification measured in query depth.\n\nAliases turn one request into many, because GraphQL lets a client alias the same field repeatedly, so a single HTTP request executes hundreds of operations; this both amplifies load and batches actions to bypass per-request rate limits. The defense is not blocking individual queries but bounding cost: limiting query depth, computing a query complexity or cost score and rejecting queries over a budget, capping aliases and the number of returned items through pagination, and adding timeouts \u2014 the server must price a query before running it.\n\nDefensively, GraphQL's expressiveness means an unbounded query is an unbounded bill, so cost analysis (depth and complexity limits, pagination, timeouts) is a core control rather than an optimization, because without it one request can exhaust the database.",w31l4:"GraphQL does not replace injection, it relays it: a resolver builds a database query, OS command, or downstream call from arguments, so SQL injection, NoSQL injection, and the rest flow through exactly as before, since the typed schema validates shape rather than safety \u2014 resolvers must parameterize like anywhere else. The signature GraphQL abuse is alias-based batching against rate limits: because one request can carry hundreds of aliased operations, an attacker brute-forces a login, a one-time code, or a coupon in a single HTTP request, defeating rate limiters that count requests rather than operations, so rate limiting must be by operation and cost.\n\nPulling the topic together, GraphQL relocates every API concern to the resolver and the query: authorization per resolver, data exposure per field, rate limiting and DoS per query cost and per operation, and reconnaissance via introspection \u2014 the single typed endpoint concentrates, rather than removes, the service-surface risks. The hardening checklist is concrete: disable introspection in production, enforce per-resolver object and field authorization, bound query depth and complexity, rate-limit by operation and cost, parameterize resolver queries, and prefer persisted (allowlisted) queries so only known shapes run.\n\nThis closes the services arc, in which SOAP, REST, and GraphQL are one lesson in three dialects: the API is the real boundary, the client is untrusted, and every guarantee must be enforced server-side per object, per field, and per cost. The next topic is serverless, and the Semester 4 synthesis.",w32l1:"Serverless (functions-as-a-service, such as AWS Lambda and Cloud Functions) runs code in short-lived, event-triggered instances with no server to manage, and the security shift is that a function is not triggered only by HTTP: it runs in response to events \u2014 a file uploaded to storage, a queue message, an email, a database change, or a scheduled timer. The attack surface therefore widens past the request, because every event source is untrusted input, and developers who validate HTTP bodies often treat a storage event or queue message as 'internal' and trust it even though an attacker who can write to that bucket or queue controls the event; event injection is the serverless face of 'don't trust input.'\n\nThe injected data flows into the same sinks as always \u2014 a filename from an upload event concatenated into a command, queue-message fields placed into a query, email content forwarded to a downstream call \u2014 so the triggers are new while the injection classes are identical to those of the injection semester. Functions are also chained, one function's output becoming another's event (upload to thumbnail to notify), so malicious data injected at the first trigger propagates through the chain, and a later function deep in the pipeline may trust data that originated outside, the way request smuggling trusted the smuggled prefix.\n\nDefensively, every event from every source, not just HTTP, should be treated as untrusted input, validated and parameterized at each function regardless of where the event came from, and an event arriving from another cloud service should never be assumed trustworthy merely because it is 'inside.'",w32l2:"In serverless there is no server to manage, so the function's IAM role \u2014 its cloud permissions \u2014 becomes the security boundary that matters most: if an attacker achieves code execution or injection in a function, what they can do is exactly what that role allows, making the role the blast radius. The dominant mistake is over-privileged functions, where a function that only needs to read one storage bucket is granted broad permissions for convenience, so one injection pivots to the whole account \u2014 the same lesson as SSRF reaching cloud metadata, in which excess privilege turns a small bug into a large breach.\n\nSecrets management shifts as well, because serverless code pulls credentials from environment variables or a secrets store, and environment variables are easy to leak through error pages, logs, or a verbose function; a leaked function environment can expose database passwords and API keys directly, so least privilege limits the damage while secret hygiene prevents the leak. Two further serverless-specific risks are denial-of-wallet, where an attacker drives massive function invocations to run up a ruinous bill (cost as the target, like GraphQL query-cost DoS), and the false comfort of statelessness, since ephemeral instances still share the account, the role, and downstream data, so a fresh container each time is not isolation from the blast radius.\n\nDefensively, every function's role should be scoped to least privilege as the single highest-leverage serverless control, secrets should be kept out of code and environment where possible and never logged, and concurrency and budget limits should be set to bound denial-of-wallet, on the assumption that any function can be compromised.",w32l3:"Cloud-native and backend-as-a-service architectures (BaaS, such as Firebase and the cloud storage SDKs) let the client talk directly to the data store with no application server in between, which removes the very tier that used to enforce authorization, so security depends entirely on declarative access rules configured on the cloud resource. The signature bug is broken or absent security rules: a storage bucket or database left world-readable or world-writable \u2014 the classic public S3 bucket \u2014 exposes all data to anyone with the URL, and a database with rules set to allow-all lets any client read or write any record; there is no server to forget the check, only a rule that was never written correctly.\n\nThis is the access-control semester with the server removed entirely, because the client is fully untrusted \u2014 it runs on the attacker's machine \u2014 so any enforcement in client code is cosmetic, and the only real boundary is the cloud resource's rules evaluated server-side by the provider, which default to nothing useful unless written. Reconnaissance finds these quickly through predictable bucket names, exposed cloud configuration in JavaScript, and public listing, turning them into bulk data exposure \u2014 the Semester 1 attacker surface meeting the storage layer \u2014 so 'no server' does not mean 'no attack surface,' it means the attack surface is the configuration.\n\nDefensively, least-privilege access rules should be written and tested on every cloud resource (deny by default, authorize per record and per object), client-side checks should never be relied upon, and audits should look for public buckets and allow-all rules, because the provider enforces the rules exactly, including the empty ones.",w32l4:"Semester 4 was one idea in many forms: the server acts on attacker-shaped input it parses, fetches, frames, rebuilds, exposes, or is triggered by \u2014 XXE parses, SSRF fetches, request smuggling frames, deserialization rebuilds, web services and GraphQL expose, and serverless is triggered \u2014 each a trust boundary the server crossed without checking. Two root causes ran through all of it: treating a parser, fetcher, or deserializer as passive when it is an interpreter that acts on structure (the injection theme escalated from strings to whole formats and protocols), and trusting position over verification \u2014 'it is internal,' 'the UI would not send that,' 'the event came from our own queue' \u2014 when the attacker controls the input regardless of where it appears to originate.\n\nThe cure family is consistent: disable dangerous capabilities (external entities, code-capable deserializers); validate and pin at the boundary (allowlist destinations, resolve-and-pin, reject ambiguous framing); enforce authorization server-side per object and per field (BOLA, resolvers, cloud rules); and bound cost and privilege (query limits, least-privilege roles) \u2014 never trusting structure or origin but verifying both. The semester also retired a comfort, that 'inside the perimeter' is a security boundary, since SSRF, smuggling, over-privileged functions, and trusted internal events all weaponize the assumption that internal traffic is safe; the boundary is wherever attacker-influenced data is acted upon, which is almost everywhere.\n\nSemester 4 covered what the server does with input it receives, and the final semester turns to the modern client and delivery \u2014 browser security boundaries, CORS and postMessage, WebSockets, plus evasion and the engagement itself \u2014 where the trust boundary moves back to the user's machine. The rule to carry forward is to identify the trust boundary, then verify across it.",w33l1:"Semester 5 moves the trust boundary back to the user's machine, where the central control is the Same-Origin Policy (SOP): the browser isolates content by origin \u2014 the (scheme, host, port) triple \u2014 so a script from one origin cannot freely read data from another, which is why a malicious site cannot simply read a user's webmail. SOP is asymmetric, and the asymmetry is the whole game: the browser blocks a cross-origin script from reading another origin's responses, but it does not block sending cross-origin requests, and it attaches the victim's cookies to those requests \u2014 the gap CSRF exploited.\n\nTwo different bugs therefore live on the two halves of SOP. CSRF abuses the send-but-cannot-read side by forcing a state-changing request, while CORS misconfiguration \u2014 this topic \u2014 abuses the read side by tricking the browser into letting a hostile origin read responses SOP would normally hide. Modern apps need legitimate cross-origin reads, such as a frontend on app.example.com calling api.example.com or a partner API, and Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) is the sanctioned mechanism for a server to opt specific origins out of SOP's read restriction \u2014 a deliberate, controlled hole.\n\nSOP is the browser's default-deny for cross-origin reads, and every client-side topic this semester concerns where that default is relaxed or bypassed. CORS is the first: a server explicitly widening who may read its responses, and getting that list wrong is the vulnerability.",w33l2:"CORS is driven by response headers the server sends, the key one being Access-Control-Allow-Origin (ACAO), which names which origin is allowed to read the response; the browser enforces it by making the request and then withholding the response body from the script unless ACAO matches the caller's origin. Enforcement is on the read, not the send, which is critical: a failing CORS check does not stop the request reaching the server or executing, it only stops the calling script from reading the response, so CORS is not an access-control or anti-CSRF mechanism \u2014 it governs who may read, nothing more.\n\nCredentials change the rules: for a request that carries cookies, the server must return ACAO set to the exact origin (not the * wildcard) and Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true, because the browser forbids wildcard-with-credentials specifically so a server cannot accidentally let any origin read authenticated responses. Some requests trigger a preflight, in which the browser sends an OPTIONS probe before the real request asking whether the method and headers are allowed and only proceeds if the server approves; preflight protects against unexpected cross-origin methods, but simple requests such as a plain GET skip it entirely.\n\nACAO is an allowlist the browser enforces on reads, and the credentialed-read path (exact-origin ACAO plus ACAC: true) is the dangerous one, because the whole vulnerability class is a server computing ACAO incorrectly.",w33l3:"The dangerous pattern is dynamic ACAO: rather than a static allowlist, the server reads the request's Origin header and reflects it back into Access-Control-Allow-Origin, and combined with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true this tells the browser that any origin \u2014 including the attacker's \u2014 may read authenticated responses, the CORS equivalent of trusting the client. Reflecting Origin is the classic misconfiguration because it is the easy way to support all of an organization's frontends, but the Origin header is attacker-controlled, so reflecting it is an allowlist of everyone, and with credentials enabled a malicious page can read the victim's logged-in data from the API.\n\nThe literal origin null is a trap, because some servers allowlist it to support sandboxed iframes or local files, yet an attacker can force a request to carry Origin: null (for example from a sandboxed iframe), so ACAO: null with credentials is exploitable too. Sloppy matching repeats earlier parser bugs: a regex or prefix/suffix check such as 'ends with example.com' is bypassed by attacker-evil-example.com or example.com.attacker.com, and starts-with or substring checks fail similarly \u2014 the same validation-by-pattern weakness seen in open-redirect and SSRF host checks.\n\nEvery one of these is the server computing ACAO from attacker-influenced input or a loose pattern, so the bug is dynamic and reflected origins, null, and fuzzy matching \u2014 not the existence of CORS \u2014 and the fix is a strict, exact, static allowlist.",w33l4:"The exploit is a pure web attack with no interception needed: the victim, logged in to the target, visits the attacker's page; the page's JavaScript makes a credentialed cross-origin request (fetch with credentials) to the target's API; the misconfigured server reflects the origin and allows credentials; and the browser hands the response to the attacker's script, which exfiltrates it, so the victim's authenticated data leaves over their own session. Scope matters, because this reads what the victim can read \u2014 profile, messages, API keys, CSRF tokens \u2014 and can chain, for example stealing a CSRF token via CORS and then forging state-changing requests, but it only works while the misconfiguration and the victim's session both hold, making it a confidentiality break on readable endpoints rather than blanket RCE.\n\nThe cure is a strict server-side allowlist: compare the Origin against an exact, static set of trusted origins and only then echo that exact origin into ACAO; never reflect arbitrary origins, never use wildcard-with-credentials, and do not allowlist null, matching the full origin exactly with no suffix, prefix, or regex shortcuts. Two supporting controls help: send Vary: Origin so caches do not serve one origin's ACAO to another, and minimize credentialed cross-origin endpoints, since defense in depth means sensitive data should not rely on CORS alone.\n\nCORS misconfiguration is SOP's read-restriction deliberately relaxed to the wrong audience \u2014 the client-side echo of trusting the client. This opens the modern-client semester; the next topic is client-side messaging (postMessage and WebSockets), where origin checks are again the boundary.",w34l1:"SOP stops cross-origin script access, but apps genuinely need windows and iframes of different origins to talk \u2014 a widget, an SSO popup, an embedded player \u2014 and window.postMessage is the sanctioned channel by which one frame sends a message and another receives it via a message event, a deliberate hole in SOP's isolation like CORS. postMessage has two origin-safety controls, one on each side: the sender passes a targetOrigin argument naming who may receive the message, and using '*' means any origin the window navigates to can read it, leaking the data if the frame is ever attacker-controlled.\n\nThe receiver gets an event with event.origin (who sent it) and event.data (the payload), and must check event.origin against an allowlist before trusting the data, because a handler that processes event.data without verifying event.origin accepts messages from any page, including the attacker's. The symmetric rule is therefore that senders specify an exact targetOrigin (never '*') for sensitive data and receivers validate event.origin (never trusting the message blindly) \u2014 the same don't-trust-the-other-side theme as CORS, now for window-to-window messaging.\n\nA message arriving via postMessage is untrusted input from a potentially hostile origin, so the message handler should be treated exactly like a server endpoint: authenticate the sender via event.origin, validate the data, and never feed it to a dangerous sink unchecked.",w34l2:"The high-impact postMessage bug is DOM-based XSS through the handler: if a message handler takes event.data and writes it to a dangerous sink \u2014 element.innerHTML, document.write, eval, or a script src \u2014 without validating event.origin and the data, any origin that can reach the window can inject script into the target's page. The attack is that the attacker's page opens or frames the target, then posts a crafted message; the target's handler, trusting any origin, writes the payload into the DOM and it executes in the target's origin \u2014 DOM XSS with postMessage as the source, and the data never touched the server.\n\nThis is why both controls matter together: validating event.origin stops hostile senders, while validating and encoding event.data stops a trusted-but-compromised sender (or a bug) from reaching the sink, so the origin check is authentication and output handling is the injection defense. The same shape recurs in widget and SDK code, where an embeddable script that listens for configuration via postMessage and eval()s it, or routes it into the DOM, turns every host page's message channel into an XSS vector, making third-party message handlers a common real-world source.\n\nThe message handler should be treated as an untrusted-input entry point: verify event.origin against an allowlist, then treat event.data as data and never code \u2014 no innerHTML or eval, using textContent or safe DOM APIs and validating structure \u2014 the same XSS rules as before with a new source.",w34l3:"WebSockets give a full-duplex, long-lived connection for real-time apps such as chat, live feeds, and dashboards, and the security catch is that the WebSocket handshake is an HTTP upgrade the browser sends with the site's cookies attached, while SOP and CORS do not govern WebSocket connections the way they govern fetch, so the browser will let any page open a socket to any host. That combination is cross-site WebSocket hijacking (CSWSH), which is CSRF for WebSockets: if the server authenticates the handshake only by cookie and does not validate the Origin header, an attacker's page can open a WebSocket to the target, the browser attaches the victim's cookies, and the socket is authenticated as the victim.\n\nUnlike CSRF, the attacker can also read, because once the cross-site socket is open and authenticated the attacker's script both sends messages and receives the replies (WebSocket is not bound by SOP read restrictions), making CSWSH read/write \u2014 exfiltrating the victim's data and performing actions, all over the victim's session. The root cause is the same as CORS and postMessage: an authenticated channel that does not verify the requesting origin, since cookies authenticate the browser, not the page driving it, so cookie-only handshake auth trusts whoever opened the socket.\n\nDefensively, the server must validate the Origin header on the WebSocket handshake against an allowlist (the browser will not) and add a CSRF-style token to the handshake, rather than relying on cookies alone, tying the connection to an unguessable value the attacker's page cannot read.",w34l4:"Three mechanisms share one rule: CORS, postMessage, and WebSockets are all sanctioned holes in the browser's origin isolation, and all three fail the same way \u2014 an authenticated or sensitive channel that does not verify the origin of the other side \u2014 so the boundary is origin and someone has to check it. Who checks differs, which is the trap: for CORS the browser enforces the server's ACAO, for postMessage the receiving script must check event.origin, and for the WebSocket handshake the server must check the Origin header (the browser will not), so mistaking 'the browser handles it' for the latter two is the common failure.\n\nCookies authenticate the browser, not the page, and across CSRF, CSWSH, and credentialed CORS the recurring root is the same: ambient cookies are attached to cross-origin requests, so a hostile page acts with the victim's authority unless the server verifies origin and adds an unguessable token. The unified defenses are therefore to validate origin explicitly on every cross-origin channel (allowlist, exact match), to avoid relying on ambient cookies for cross-origin auth (add tokens), and to treat any cross-origin message \u2014 a CORS response consumer, postMessage data, or a WebSocket frame \u2014 as untrusted input feeding the normal injection defenses.\n\nThis completes the cross-origin trio. The next topic moves inside the origin to client-side storage and workers, where the question shifts from who may talk across origins to what persists on the client and who can read it.",w35l1:"The browser gives pages persistent client-side storage \u2014 localStorage (persists until cleared), sessionStorage (per-tab, cleared on close), and IndexedDB (a larger structured/object store for bigger client-side data) \u2014 all scoped to the origin and all fully readable and writable by any JavaScript running in that origin. The same no-HttpOnly fact applies to IndexedDB, and storing a payload in localStorage or IndexedDB that a later page load reads into a sink (innerHTML, eval) is second-order DOM XSS \u2014 persistent client-side execution that never touches the server. The security crux is that web storage has no equivalent of a cookie's HttpOnly flag: there is no way to mark a stored value off-limits to scripts because reading it is the API, so any script in the origin, including one injected via XSS, can read everything in it.\n\nThe direct consequence is that a session token or JWT stored in localStorage is stolen by a single line of injected JavaScript, so XSS that would be limited against an HttpOnly cookie becomes full account takeover when the token sits in web storage. Web storage also is not sent automatically with requests (unlike cookies), which is why developers use it for tokens to sidestep CSRF, but that trade swaps an automatic CSRF risk for a script-readable XSS risk, and XSS is the more common and more total compromise.\n\nDefensively, everything in web storage should be treated as readable by any script in the origin, so secrets, session tokens, and anything sensitive should not be stored there; if XSS is possible (it usually is somewhere), web-storage tokens are the prize, and while the real fix is preventing XSS, the storage choice changes the blast radius.",w35l2:"Session-token storage is a real trade-off between two attack classes: an HttpOnly cookie cannot be read by JavaScript so it survives XSS but is sent automatically (the CSRF exposure), while a localStorage token is never auto-sent (no CSRF) but is script-readable (XSS steals it), so the choice is which risk to carry. The modern answer leans to cookies with the right flags \u2014 HttpOnly (no script access), Secure (HTTPS only), and SameSite (Lax or Strict, blocking the cross-site auto-send CSRF needs) \u2014 because SameSite addresses the cookie's CSRF weakness while leaving its XSS resistance intact, capturing much of both protections.\n\nThe reasoning is blast radius: XSS is more common and more catastrophic than CSRF because it runs arbitrary script in the origin, so a defense that keeps the token unreadable to script (HttpOnly cookie) limits the worst case better than one that exposes it (localStorage), and one should not optimize away the smaller risk by enlarging the bigger one. If a token must be in JavaScript, such as a single-page app calling a cross-origin API with bearer tokens, exposure should be minimized through short lifetimes, refresh rotation, and rigorous XSS prevention, because the token's confidentiality then depends entirely on no script injection ever succeeding.\n\nDefensively, HttpOnly+Secure+SameSite cookies are preferred for session tokens, web storage is reserved for non-sensitive data, and storage choice is recognized as a containment decision whose ultimate backstop is the origin's XSS posture.",w35l3:"A service worker is the most powerful kind of web worker \u2014 a background script the browser runs separate from any page \u2014 and where an ordinary web worker only runs off-main-thread computation with no DOM access (so its abuse is resource-driven: attacker JavaScript spinning up workers for in-browser DDoS or distributed password cracking on the victim's CPU), a service worker can intercept and respond to the origin's network requests \u2014 the engine behind offline apps, caching, and push \u2014 making it a programmable proxy between the origin's pages and the network. That power is the risk: if an attacker can register a service worker (typically via XSS, since registration is a JavaScript call), they install a persistent, page-independent foothold in the origin that survives navigation and tab closes and keeps running until explicitly unregistered, so whereas XSS is usually a one-page event, a malicious worker makes it durable.\n\nA hostile service worker can intercept every request in its scope, serving poisoned responses from its cache (client-side cache poisoning), capturing data the user submits, and continuing to exfiltrate long after the original XSS payload is gone, turning a transient injection into ongoing control of what the origin's pages see. Service workers are constrained \u2014 same-origin, HTTPS-only, and limited to a URL scope \u2014 which bounds but does not remove the danger, because the bound is the origin, exactly what one is trying to protect; the constraints stop cross-origin abuse, not abuse within the compromised origin.\n\nDefensively, the priority is preventing the XSS that allows registration (the precondition), with defense in depth from a strict Content Security Policy limiting injected script and from monitoring and scoping registration, and an unexpected service worker should be treated as a persistent compromise rather than a cache quirk.",w35l4:"The unifying lesson is that nothing on the client is trustworthy or private from the user or from script running in the origin: web storage is script-readable, service workers can be hijacked, and all client-side state lives on a machine the user and any injected code fully control, so the client is not a vault. Two rules follow \u2014 never store secrets or trust client-side data, because tokens, prices, roles, and feature flags on the client are readable and tamperable; and never enforce security on the client, because validation, authorization, and business rules must be re-checked server-side, client checks being advisory at best, which is the access-control lesson restated for storage.\n\nThis connects the whole semester: SOP, CORS, postMessage, and WebSockets concern who may cross between origins, while storage and workers concern what sits inside an origin and how injected script abuses it, and both reduce to the same fact \u2014 the browser environment is shared with the attacker the moment XSS exists. This is why XSS is the master client-side vulnerability: it does not merely deface a page but reads web storage, registers service workers, drives cross-origin channels, and acts as the user, so most of the semester's worst outcomes are 'XSS, then...', and while storage and channel hardening shrink the blast radius, XSS prevention removes the detonator.\n\nDefensively, secrets and enforcement belong on the server, sensitive client-side state should be minimized, and XSS prevention should be prioritized as the highest-leverage client control. The next topic is UI redressing \u2014 clickjacking \u2014 where the attack needs no script at all, just the victim's own clicks.",w36l1:"UI redressing \u2014 clickjacking \u2014 attacks the user's intent rather than the code: the attacker loads the target site in a transparent iframe positioned over their own page, so the victim sees the attacker's harmless-looking UI (a game, a 'claim prize' button) while their click actually lands on the invisible target framed on top. The crucial property is that the framed target is the real site, loaded with the victim's cookies and session, so a click passes through to a genuine, authenticated action \u2014 change a setting, confirm a payment, grant a permission \u2014 performed by the victim on the real application, which sees a legitimate authenticated click.\n\nIt needs no script injection and no CSRF token theft, and it sidesteps CSRF tokens entirely because the request is a real interaction with the real page (the token is present and valid) \u2014 the victim genuinely, if unknowingly, performed the action, making it an attack on perception. The setup is alignment and opacity: CSS makes the target iframe transparent (opacity 0) and positions the sensitive button exactly under the decoy the victim is enticed to click, sometimes choreographing multiple steps to walk the victim through a flow.\n\nThe root cause is that a sensitive page allows itself to be framed by another origin \u2014 the whole vulnerability is that the page can be loaded in someone else's frame \u2014 and the defense is telling the browser who may frame it.",w36l2:"Clickjacking's impact is bounded by what a single (or few) authenticated click can accomplish without typed input, so high-value targets are one-click state changes: delete account, authorize this app (OAuth consent), confirm transfer, make profile public, or toggling a security setting \u2014 anything dangerous that needs only a click. Likejacking is the classic mass example, framing a social like, follow, or share button to inflate engagement or spread content; individually low-harm but illustrating that any valuable click-only action is a target and scale makes it profitable.\n\nBeyond clicks, redressing extends to other input: drag-and-drop clickjacking can trick a victim into dragging data (or a token displayed on the framed page) into an attacker-controlled field to exfiltrate information, while cursorjacking and keystroke redirection misdirect where input actually goes \u2014 the theme being that the victim's input is retargeted. The limits matter, because clickjacking cannot read cross-origin page contents (SOP still holds, so the frame is opaque to the attacker's script) and cannot easily perform multi-field typed operations; it drives the victim's clicks against the real UI without reading the response.\n\nDefensively, clickjacking impact is assessed by asking what a single authenticated click can do on a given page, since sensitive one-click actions \u2014 consent, delete, transfer, privacy toggles \u2014 are the real risk and the reason framing protection matters most there.",w36l3:"The defense is a response header declaring framing policy. The modern control is Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors, which lists the origins allowed to frame the page \u2014 'none' forbids all framing, 'self' allows same-origin only, or an explicit allowlist \u2014 and the browser enforces it, refusing to render the page in a disallowed frame. The older control is X-Frame-Options (DENY or SAMEORIGIN), which still works in legacy browsers but is coarse (no allowlist beyond same-origin) and effectively superseded by frame-ancestors, so both should be sent for coverage while frame-ancestors is treated as authoritative and flexible.\n\nJavaScript frame-busting is not a reliable defense: the old 'if (top != self) top.location = self.location' scripts are bypassable, since sandboxed iframes disable the navigation and various tricks defeat them, so script should never be relied on to prevent framing because it is a client-side check the attacker's frame controls. SameSite cookies are a partial, incidental help, because SameSite=Lax/Strict means the framed cross-site request may not carry the session cookie so the click is not authenticated, mitigating some clickjacking as a side effect \u2014 but the correct, intentional fix is frame-ancestors rather than cookie policy.\n\nDefensively, Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors (plus X-Frame-Options for legacy) should be set on every page that performs sensitive actions, defaulting to 'none' or 'self,' never trusting JS frame-busting; the fix is declaring framing policy to the browser, the same shape as CORS and postMessage \u2014 being explicit about who may interact with you.",w36l4:"The modern-client half of Semester 5 maps one terrain: the browser is a shared environment where the developer's code, the user, other origins, and any injected script all coexist, so every client-side feature is a trust boundary, and origin isolation, client state, and the user's own intent are each something an attacker tries to cross. Three boundary types were covered: cross-origin channels (CORS, postMessage, WebSockets) concern who may read or talk across origins, fixed by validating origin and not trusting ambient cookies; client state (web storage, service workers) concerns what lives in the origin and who can read or abuse it, fixed by keeping secrets and enforcement server-side; and the user's intent (clickjacking) is fixed by declaring framing policy.\n\nTwo root causes recur from the whole course: trusting the client \u2014 storage, client-side checks, ambient cookies \u2014 restated for the browser, and not verifying the other party \u2014 origin on channels, framing origin for clickjacking \u2014 the client-side form of authenticating before trusting. XSS sits above all of it, reading storage, driving channels, registering workers, and acting as the user, so the modern-client surface is both a set of distinct bugs and a force-multiplier for injection, and while hardening each boundary limits damage, preventing XSS prevents the chaining.\n\nDefensively, the browser is treated as adversary-adjacent: validate origins, keep secrets and enforcement server-side, declare framing policy, and prevent XSS \u2014 the modern-client checklist. The midterm covers Weeks 33\u201336, after which the semester turns outward to evasion (beating filters and WAFs) and the engagement itself.",w37l1:"A security filter \u2014 input validation, a denylist, a WAF rule \u2014 works by inspecting input and deciding whether it is malicious before the real interpreter (SQL engine, HTML parser, shell) sees it, which makes the filter a second parser that must perfectly predict how the real interpreter will read the same bytes. The entire evasion class is one idea: any disagreement between the filter's reading and the interpreter's reading is a bypass, so if the filter thinks a string is harmless but the interpreter executes it as a command, the attacker wins \u2014 the same two-parsers-disagree root as request smuggling and mutation XSS, now filter versus interpreter.\n\nThis is why blocklists are structurally weak: a blocklist must enumerate every dangerous form an attacker might use, but interpreters accept a huge space of equivalent encodings, syntaxes, and quirks, so there is almost always a variant the blocklist did not list but the interpreter still understands. Allowlists invert the odds, because defining what is permitted (this field is digits 0\u20139, max length 10) and rejecting everything else means the attacker must find an allowed input that is also malicious, which is usually impossible \u2014 the asymmetry is the whole point: enumerate good, not bad.\n\nDefensively, any filter should be treated as a fallible predictor of the interpreter, allowlists preferred over blocklists, and a WAF or denylist never treated as the primary fix; the real cure is the context-correct defense at the sink (parameterized queries, output encoding), with filtering as a bonus layer rather than the boundary.",w37l2:"The most common bypass is encoding: the attacker encodes the payload so the filter sees a benign-looking string, but a layer between the filter and the interpreter decodes it back to the live payload, so the filter and interpreter are looking at the same request through different decoding stages. Web stacks decode repeatedly, which is the opening, and URL-encoding (%27 for a quote), double URL-encoding (%2527, decoded twice), HTML entities, unicode escapes, hex, and base64 each let an attacker hide a keyword or metacharacter from a filter that does not decode the same way the downstream layer will.\n\nDouble-encoding is the classic: the WAF decodes once and sees %27 (literal text, harmless), passes it, and then the application server decodes again to ', the live quote, so the payload reconstitutes after the filter has approved it \u2014 the number of decode passes is the disagreement. The root fix is canonicalization before validation: decode and normalize input to one canonical form first, then validate that single form, and reject input that is still ambiguous or multiply-encoded, because validating before fully decoding is validating the wrong string.\n\nDefensively, un-normalized input should never be validated; instead, canonicalize to a single representation, then apply allowlist validation, and ensure the downstream layer does not perform surprise extra decodes \u2014 encoding bypasses are decode-order disagreements, so the fix is to remove the disagreement.",w37l3:"Even without encoding, interpreters accept many equivalent syntaxes, and a filter matching one exact form misses the others: case variation (SeLeCt), inline comments (UN/**/ION in SQL), extra whitespace or newlines, and alternate quoting all produce the same execution while defeating a naive signature. SQL is the rich example, where comments split keywords (UNION becomes UN/**/ION), case is ignored (uNiOn SeLeCt), and equivalent functions or operators substitute for blocked ones, so a denylist for the literal string 'UNION SELECT' is bypassed by any of these while the database executes them identically.\n\nThe same leniency exists everywhere \u2014 HTML and JavaScript for XSS (mixed-case tags, optional quotes, event-handler variety, whitespace inside tags) and shells for command injection (quotes, $IFS, brace expansion, variable tricks) \u2014 so each interpreter's tolerance is exactly the attacker's bypass surface. This is why signature matching is a losing game: it enumerates a forgiving grammar's infinite equivalent expressions, and the interpreter's permissiveness, present to help developers, is what makes denylist signatures incomplete by construction.\n\nDefensively, the answer is not to out-enumerate the grammar but to use the structural defense that makes the payload's syntax irrelevant \u2014 parameterized queries (data never parsed as SQL), context-aware output encoding (XSS), and avoiding shells or using argument arrays (command injection) \u2014 removing the interpreter's exposure rather than pattern-matching its dialects.",w37l4:"A Web Application Firewall inspects HTTP traffic and blocks requests matching known-malicious patterns (signatures) or statistical anomalies, and it is useful \u2014 buying time against known exploits, blocking noisy automated scanning, and virtually patching a known CVE while a real fix ships \u2014 but what it is determines what it cannot do. A WAF is a generic filter that does not know the application's logic or context, so it must guess which inputs are dangerous, and encoding and syntactic variation give attackers endless ways to phrase a payload the WAF did not anticipate, so a determined attacker bypasses a WAF while it stops the lazy and the known.\n\nA WAF is therefore defense in depth, not a fix: the vulnerability still exists behind it and the WAF just makes one expression of it harder, so treating the WAF as the remediation ('we are protected, the WAF blocks SQLi') leaves the real bug live for the first bypass, a dangerous false comfort. This whole evasion topic is the two-parsers-disagree spine applied to defenses, because the WAF and the app parse the request differently (decode passes, parameter handling, body parsing) and that mismatch is the bypass, whereas securing at the sink, in the app, removes the second parser and its disagreement entirely.\n\nDefensively, a WAF should run for what it is good at (known-exploit blocking, rate limiting, buying time) while vulnerabilities are fixed at the code and sink level as the real remediation: prefer allowlists, canonicalize before validating, and never let 'the WAF will catch it' substitute for the structural defense. The next topic covers advanced bypasses that exploit the WAF-versus-app mismatch directly.",w38l1:"HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) sends the same parameter name more than once \u2014 ?id=1&id=2 \u2014 and exploits that there is no single standard for which value wins, since different layers resolve duplicates differently: some take the first, some the last, some concatenate them, and some return an array. That inconsistency is the bypass, because if the WAF inspects the first id and the application uses the last (or vice versa), the attacker puts a benign value where the filter looks and the payload where the app reads, so the same request means different things to the two parsers \u2014 the evasion theme applied to parameter parsing.\n\nHPP also splits a payload across duplicates to dodge signatures: a value that would match a rule as one string is broken into id=UNION&id=SELECT, and if a downstream layer concatenates duplicate parameters the full payload reassembles past the filter that saw only fragments. The behavior varies by stack (language, framework, server, WAF), so HPP is partly reconnaissance \u2014 probe which occurrence each layer honors, then position the payload accordingly \u2014 a discovery-then-exploit pattern rather than a fixed trick.\n\nDefensively, duplicate parameters should be normalized to one defined behavior before validation (reject duplicates or canonicalize), the filter and the app should resolve parameters identically, and defense at the sink ensures parameter-parsing quirks cannot reach an interpreter; the bug is the disagreement between filter and app on parameter handling.",w38l2:"The deepest bypasses move below the parameter into how the request itself is parsed: if the WAF and the origin disagree about request structure \u2014 body length, chunking, content type, multipart boundaries \u2014 the WAF may inspect a different request than the one the app assembles, which is request smuggling's family aimed at evasion. Transfer-Encoding: chunked is a classic lever, because a WAF that does not fully reassemble chunked bodies (or handles them differently than the origin) can be fed a payload split across chunks or hidden by malformed chunk sizing, so the WAF sees fragments while the origin sees the assembled attack.\n\nContent-Type and charset confusion bypass body inspection, since declaring an unexpected type or an alternate charset like UTF-7 or UTF-16 can make the WAF parse the body wrongly or skip it while the app decodes it into the live payload, and multipart form tricks \u2014 duplicate boundaries, ambiguous parts \u2014 similarly desynchronize which bytes each side treats as the field. The unifying mechanism is a parser differential: any place the WAF and the application can be made to read the request structurally differently is a bypass, exactly like CL.TE smuggling but used to slip a payload past inspection rather than to poison a connection.\n\nDefensively, parser diversity should be minimized (the same parsing and normalization in WAF and app), ambiguous or malformed framing rejected (bad chunking, unexpected content types, duplicate boundaries), and the WAF never relied upon to parse the request identically to the origin \u2014 the surest fix remaining the sink defense the WAF was only shielding.",w38l3:"Context-specific payloads bypass a WAF that normalizes for the wrong context: a WAF may neutralize HTML-context XSS but miss a payload crafted for a JavaScript-string or attribute context, and SQL payloads tuned to a specific database's dialect dodge generic rules, because the WAF guesses the context while the app's actual sink defines it. Mutation and obscure features extend the surface \u2014 null bytes truncating a filter's view, overlong UTF-8, mixing techniques (encode plus comment plus case), and database or engine-specific functions the rule set never enumerated \u2014 each another expression the generic filter did not predict.\n\nThe pattern across evasion II is that the attacker maximizes the gap between the WAF's generic model and the app's specific behavior: every WAF rule is a generalization while every app has exact context, so evasion lives in that distance, which is why a WAF can be tuned but never completed. This is the practical limit of filtering restated \u2014 one can raise the cost of an attack (real value against opportunists), but a context-aware attacker with knowledge of the stack will find the unmodeled case, so cost-raising is legitimate while relying on it as the boundary is not.\n\nDefensively, the WAF should raise attacker cost and block the known, tuned to the stack to shrink the gap, while the sink defense is treated as the boundary: context-correct encoding, parameterized queries, and strict allowlists make the bypass irrelevant because there is no interpreter exposure left to reach.",w38l4:"Two weeks of evasion reduce to one statement: every bypass is a parser differential \u2014 filter versus interpreter (encoding, syntax), WAF versus app (HPP, chunked/charset, multipart), and generic-rule versus exact-context (mutation) \u2014 in each case two components reading the same bytes differently while the attacker lives in the gap. The cure family is therefore consistent: shrink and remove the differentials by canonicalizing input to one form before validating, making the filter and the app parse identically (or removing the redundant parser), rejecting ambiguous framing, and decisively defending at the sink so no parser quirk reaches an interpreter \u2014 removing the second reader or making its reading irrelevant.\n\nThis reframes the WAF correctly as a cost-raiser and an early-warning / known-exploit layer with real operational value (buying time, blocking scanners, virtual patching) that is nonetheless structurally a guesser, so 'we have a WAF' is a monitoring statement, not a remediation statement. It also closes the technical arc of the course: from injection (data treated as code) through the two-parsers spine (smuggling, mutation XSS, SOP bypass) to evasion (defeating the filters meant to compensate for unfixed sinks), the recurring fix has always been the same \u2014 handle untrusted input structurally at the boundary rather than pattern-matching it.\n\nDefensively, one cannot filter their way to safety; it is engineered at the sink. The next topic shifts from individual bugs to the engagement \u2014 chaining vulnerabilities into real impact and the methodology of a professional test \u2014 turning everything so far into findings.",w39l1:"Real-world severity comes from chains, not single bugs: a vulnerability rated 'low' in isolation \u2014 an open redirect, a verbose error, a self-XSS, an info leak \u2014 often becomes critical when linked to others, so severity is contextual, depending on what the bug enables next rather than on a generic label. The canonical example is an info leak exposing an internal hostname, SSRF reaching it, the internal service being unauthenticated, and its admin function yielding RCE, where no single step is critical alone but the chain is, because attackers think in graphs of capability rather than isolated findings.\n\nChaining also upgrades 'unexploitable' bugs: self-XSS (which only attacks oneself) becomes real when chained with a login-CSRF or a content-injection that delivers it to a victim, and an open redirect becomes serious as the pivot that makes an OAuth token theft or a phishing chain land \u2014 context creates impact. This reframes triage, because dismissing low-severity findings in isolation is how breaches happen, since the attacker's job is precisely to assemble them, so a defender should ask of every minor bug what it becomes when combined with the others present.\n\nDefensively, findings should be assessed as a connected graph rather than a checklist of severities, and low-severity bugs that are chain enablers (open redirects, info leaks, SSRF primitives) should be fixed with the seriousness of what they unlock, because the chain's impact is the real risk.",w39l2:"A professional test is a repeatable methodology rather than random payload-throwing, with phases: reconnaissance (map the attack surface), enumeration (identify technologies, endpoints, parameters, roles), exploitation (turn surface into access), and post-exploitation (escalate privileges, pivot, assess real impact). Reconnaissance is disproportionately decisive, which is why the course opened with it, because one can only test what one has found, so coverage of the attack surface \u2014 subdomains, endpoints, parameters, APIs, old versions \u2014 bounds everything after, and most missed vulnerabilities are missed surface, not missed payloads.\n\nThe phases loop rather than running once, because each new access reveals new surface to re-enumerate (an internal network after SSRF, an admin panel after privilege escalation), making methodology iterative \u2014 exploit, then recon again from the new vantage point \u2014 so the kill chain is a spiral, not a line. Methodology is also what makes a test complete and defensible, since systematic coverage of every endpoint, parameter, role, and trust boundary separates a thorough assessment from a lucky finding and lets the tester state confidently what was and was not tested.\n\nDefensively, the same phases guide protection: reduce attack surface (recon), minimize information disclosure (enumeration), defend sinks (exploitation), and segment with least privilege to limit pivoting (post-exploitation) \u2014 the attacker's methodology is the defender's checklist, run from the other side.",w39l3:"What separates a penetration test from a crime is authorization: every technique in this course is legal only within an explicit, written agreement that defines what may be tested, how, and when, so 'authorized testing' is not a vibe but a signed scope, and acting outside it is unlawful regardless of intent. Scope defines the targets and boundaries \u2014 which domains, IPs, applications, and accounts are in scope and which are explicitly out (third-party services, production data, certain destructive tests) \u2014 while rules of engagement add the how: allowed techniques, testing windows, rate limits, data-handling rules, and escalation contacts.\n\nSome actions need extra care or explicit sign-off, including anything that could cause downtime (denial of service, automated scanning at scale), anything touching real user data, and pivoting beyond the initial target, because being 'in scope' for the app does not automatically authorize exfiltrating real customer records to prove a point. This is exactly why Vector's kinetic exercises are simulated and sandboxed \u2014 to practice the reasoning without live targets or shipped offensive tooling \u2014 since the skill being built is judgment about systems one is authorized to assess, and the legal and ethical frame is part of the craft rather than a footnote.\n\nDefensively, and as the tester's duty, authorization should be confirmed in writing before testing, scope respected, harm minimized (proving impact without causing it where possible), any accessed data protected, and a clear channel maintained to report critical findings immediately rather than continuing to exploit.",w39l4:"The final translation a tester makes is from technical bug to business risk: a finding's value to the organization is not 'XSS on /profile' but 'an attacker can take over any user's account, including admins, leading to data breach and regulatory exposure,' so impact is told in the language of the business, grounded in the technical proof. Demonstrating impact responsibly is a skill \u2014 proving the vulnerability is real and showing what it could do without actually causing the harm, so a proof-of-concept that reads one's own test account's data demonstrates the IDOR without dumping the customer database \u2014 maximum clarity, minimum damage.\n\nRisk is impact and likelihood together, so a critical-impact bug behind three unlikely preconditions may rank below a medium-impact bug that is trivially exploitable and exposed, and communicating that nuance rather than a raw severity score is what makes a report actionable for those deciding what to fix first. This is the bridge to the final week, because everything the course taught \u2014 recon, the bug classes, chaining, evasion \u2014 exists to answer a defender's question of what is actually at risk, how, and what should be done about it, and the deliverable that answers that is the report.\n\nDefensively, findings should be consumed as risk decisions rather than trophies: prioritized by real impact and likelihood in context, remediated at the root with the structural defenses taught all course, and a tester who explains business impact and remediation valued over one who only collects criticals. The next topic is writing the report.",w4l1:"The Same-Origin Policy is better pictured as a wall with sanctioned doors than as a solid barrier. CORS, postMessage, document.domain, and legacy mechanisms such as JSONP all exist to let origins interact deliberately. Understanding those doors is the prerequisite for finding the ones left unlatched, because real-world bypasses almost never come from the policy being absent.\n\nThe vocabulary matters. An exception is a deliberate, opted-in relaxation; a bypass is an unintended cross-origin read or action the model never meant to permit. Both produce the same observable effect \u2014 code in origin A touches origin B \u2014 but one is configured and the other is a defect, and only the latter is a finding.\n\nMost bypasses cluster into a handful of classes: a misconfigured exception such as a CORS policy that reflects any Origin; a confused deputy where the browser acts on the wrong origin's behalf; a parser or normalization mismatch where two components disagree about what the origin is; and a UI or context confusion where the human is misled about which origin they are on. The attacker's opening move on any target is therefore an inventory of the doors \u2014 which exceptions are enabled, how loosely, and whether any layer derives the origin differently.\n\nFor a defender, the discipline is to treat every enabled relaxation as owned attack surface and to audit each with a single question: could this be driven by an origin I did not intend? Answering it honestly turns a stretched exception back into a wall.",w4l2:"Old vulnerabilities are worth studying even when the specific bug is long patched, because the detail changes while the shape recurs. Two classic Same-Origin Policy bypasses illustrate the shape rather than offer anything to reuse. In CVE-2007-0981, a Firefox-era flaw, the browser could be confused about a URI's host so that content was treated as belonging to a different origin than it actually did \u2014 a parsing and normalization disagreement that promoted attacker-controlled content into a trusted origin's context. In CVE-2011-3246, a WebKit context-handling flaw, script could run with the wrong origin association, because the engine and the security check disagreed about which origin owned the executing context.\n\nThe common root is the important part: in neither case did SOP 'turn off.' The policy was enforced the entire time. The bug was that one layer computed the origin one way and another layer computed it differently, and the gap between those two answers became a cross-origin read. This is precisely the parser-disagreement instinct developed earlier in the course \u2014 front-end versus back-end in request smuggling, filter depth versus sink depth in double encoding \u2014 now pointed at origins.\n\nThe research takeaway follows directly: when hunting SOP issues, do not look for a missing check, look for two components that derive the origin independently. Any point where the origin is computed or associated more than once is a point where the two computations can disagree, and that disagreement is the vulnerability.",w4l3:"Site isolation exists to answer a worst-case threat model. If the renderer process running untrusted web code is fully compromised, or if a hardware side channel such as Spectre lets that process speculatively read its own memory, then any other site's data sitting in the same process is exposed. The defense is structural rather than clever: give each site its own operating-system process, so that another site's data was never co-resident to be read in the first place.\n\nTwo qualifications define its scope. First, the unit of isolation is the site \u2014 the registrable domain plus scheme \u2014 which is broader than the origin. sub.a.com and a.com are different origins but the same site, so cross-origin-same-site data may still share a process; site isolation hardens the enforcement of SOP without replacing SOP's finer granularity. Second, it is specifically a defense against memory disclosure. It stops a compromised renderer or a speculative read from reaching another site's HTML, cookies, or storage, because that data is elsewhere.\n\nWhat it does not address is equally important: SOP-level logic bugs, CORS misconfiguration, phishing, and network-position attacks all deliver data through legitimate channels, and no process boundary helps with them. The practical consequence for a tester is to treat site isolation as a strong mitigation for one frightening class of bug while continuing to reason about a target's access control at the origin and policy layer, where the everyday vulnerabilities actually live. 'It's isolated' is not a substitute for 'the policy is correct.'",w4l4:"The fitting close to a chapter on the browser's security model is to attack the assumption the entire model rests on: that the user knows which origin they are interacting with, and learns it by reading the address bar. The address bar is the human trust anchor, and address-bar spoofing attacks it directly while every policy control continues to function perfectly.\n\nThe primitives vary. A navigation or rendering race can display the target's URL while attacker content remains on screen. A slow-load or interstitial trick can leave a trusted URL showing over the wrong page. A long, right-to-left, or Unicode-laden URL can push the real host out of the visible field. The most durable variant does not even lie: an internationalized-domain (IDN) homograph registers a domain whose characters are visually identical to a trusted brand \u2014 a Cyrillic letter standing in for a Latin one \u2014 so the bar is perfectly honest and the human's eyes are deceived.\n\nThe browser's anti-phishing layer is part of the same attack surface. Reputation and blocklist checks can be evaded with a brand-new domain, with cloaking that serves a benign page to the safety crawler while showing the attack to victims, or with a redirect laundered through a trusted host; defeat that layer and the last warning never fires. A convincing spoof plus a clean reputation check is a complete phish.\n\nThe lesson for defenders and users alike is that the address bar and the anti-phishing layer are security UI with their own bypass surface. 'It shows the right URL' is necessary but not sufficient, and security decisions should never be rendered somewhere a spoof can reach them.",w40l1:"The finding is the atomic unit of a report \u2014 one vulnerability, written so a reader can understand, reproduce, and fix it \u2014 and a complete finding has a clear title, a severity/risk rating, the affected component (URL, parameter, endpoint), a description of the flaw, its business impact, exact reproduction steps, remediation guidance, and references. Reproduction steps are the heart: precise, ordered, copy-pasteable instructions (request, payload, expected versus actual result) that let an engineer reproduce the issue without the tester, because a finding that cannot be reproduced will be disputed or ignored, so reproducibility is what makes it actionable.\n\nImpact and remediation are what the reader actually acts on: impact states what an attacker gains in business terms, and remediation gives a concrete, root-cause fix ('use parameterized queries here,' not 'sanitize input'), ideally with the specific code or config change. Tone and accuracy matter professionally \u2014 precise, neutral, evidence-backed, no overclaiming \u2014 because a report that exaggerates severity or includes unverified findings loses the reader's trust and undermines the real issues, so one writes what was proved, rates it honestly, and separates fact from speculation.\n\nDefensively, a good finding is reproducible, impact-explained, and root-cause-remediated, and when receiving one a team should prioritize by its honest risk rating and fix the root cause it names, while pushing back on findings that lack reproduction or inflate severity.",w40l2:"Risk rating turns a finding into a priority, and the common framework is CVSS (the Common Vulnerability Scoring System), which produces a 0\u201310 score from metrics such as attack vector, complexity, privileges required, and impact to confidentiality, integrity, and availability, giving a shared, comparable baseline across findings and organizations. But a score is a starting point, not the answer, because CVSS base scores ignore context: an internet-facing critical on unauthenticated functionality with sensitive data is very different from the same bug behind authentication on a low-value internal tool, so environmental and contextual adjustment is the real work.\n\nRisk is impact and likelihood together, and good rating communicates both, since a trivially exploitable, exposed medium often deserves attention before a critical gated behind unlikely preconditions \u2014 rating is a prioritization tool for limited remediation capacity, a defensible ordering rather than a number for its own sake. Rate honestly and consistently, because inflating everything to 'critical' destroys the signal prioritization depends on while under-rating buries real risk, and consistency across the report (the same criteria applied to every finding) is what lets a reader trust the ordering and plan the fixes.\n\nDefensively, use CVSS for a comparable baseline, then adjust for the environment (exposure, data sensitivity, compensating controls) and communicate impact and likelihood rather than just a number, because the goal is a defensible remediation order the team can act on.",w40l3:"A report serves two audiences in one document: the executive summary speaks to leadership and risk owners \u2014 what was tested, the overall risk posture, key themes, and business implications, in plain language without jargon \u2014 while the technical findings speak to engineers with full detail, reproduction, and remediation, so it is the same facts at two altitudes. The executive summary is often the only part leadership reads, so it must stand alone, conveying the risk story and the decisions it demands (what to fix, what it costs, what is accepted) without requiring the technical section, because burying the headline risk deep in the document is a communication failure rather than the reader's fault.\n\nRemediation guidance should be prioritized and root-cause oriented, grouping findings by underlying cause (for example, missing output encoding across several endpoints), giving the structural fix, and ordering by risk so the team spends limited capacity on the highest-impact changes first \u2014 a report is a plan, not just a list. The report sits in a lifecycle \u2014 scoping (agree targets and rules), testing (the methodology), reporting, remediation, and retest (verify the fixes actually closed the issues) \u2014 so the engagement is not done at the report but when the risks are confirmed resolved.\n\nDefensively, the executive summary is read as the risk decision and the findings as the work plan, fixes are made by root cause and priority, and retesting is always performed, because an unverified fix is an assumption \u2014 the report exists to drive remediation, not to sit in a drawer.",w40l4:"One idea underlies the entire course: software breaks at trust boundaries \u2014 the lines where data crosses from a place you do not control to a place you do \u2014 and every vulnerability studied is a boundary where untrusted input was treated as trusted, whether as code, a command, identity, structure, or intent. Two laws recur everywhere: do not trust the client or any input, because the browser, the request, the cookie, the event, the serialized blob, and the API caller are all attacker-influenceable, so authority and validation must live server-side; and two parsers must not disagree, because request smuggling, mutation XSS, SOP/CORS bypass, deserialization, and WAF evasion are all components reading the same bytes differently, with the attacker living in that gap.\n\nThe defenses rhyme as much as the bugs: treat data as data, not code (parameterized queries, output encoding, data-only formats); verify, do not assume (authenticate origin and identity, check authorization per object at the server); and reduce and disable (least privilege, disable dangerous capabilities, shrink attack surface) \u2014 the fix is structural, at the boundary, never a blocklist bolted on after. The security mindset is the durable takeaway: for any feature, ask where the trust boundary is, what crosses it, who controls that input, and what the component will do with it \u2014 that question, not a payload list, is what generalizes to vulnerabilities and technologies this course never named.\n\nThe journey ran from recon to reporting across the whole web stack \u2014 mapping surface, the injection family, access and identity, the server's data and services, the modern client, evasion, and the engagement \u2014 and the rule to carry above all is to identify the trust boundary, then verify across it, structurally and server-side, every time. That is the craft.",w5l1:"Reconnaissance rests on a single premise: a target's real attack surface is everything it exposes, not the handful of hosts its own team keeps in mind. Acquisitions, abandoned projects, and shadow IT leave behind machines nobody decommissions, and the asset that ends up compromised is usually the one missing from the official inventory. Recon, in effect, builds the inventory the defender never finished.\n\nThat is why the work starts from ownership rather than from known hostnames. An organization controls one or more Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs) and the IP prefixes announced under them, and that public routing and registration data draws the outer boundary of what the organization is responsible for. From a single known domain you can pivot to its IP, to the netblock that IP sits in, and to the ASN that announces the block; from the ASN you expand back out to every prefix it covers, then sweep those prefixes for hosts that answer.\n\nThe defender's response is to run the identical exercise inwardly. Continuous asset discovery seeded from your own ASNs and blocks finds the forgotten host before an outsider does, which is the only way to remove the free entry point. You cannot protect what you have not enumerated, and the attacker's first advantage is simply having enumerated more thoroughly than the owner.",w5l2:"A single IP address is rarely a single website. Shared hosting and name-based virtual hosting routinely place many domains on one address, so an IP should be read as a neighborhood rather than a house. Reverse IP lookup converts an address into the list of domains co-located on it, and that list expands the surface: from one in-scope host you surface its infrastructure neighbors, any of which \u2014 if weak \u2014 can become the route into the shared environment.\n\nRecon at this stage is fundamentally a scale problem. A real estate involves thousands of addresses and millions of candidate names, which is why lookups and probes are threaded and run concurrently; a serial sweep simply never finishes inside a useful window. Concurrency is a means, not a flourish.\n\nTwo judgments keep the breadth honest. First, scope: co-located domains may belong to unrelated tenants and fall outside the engagement, so discovery is not authorization. Second, noise: aggressive concurrency trips rate limits and intrusion detection and can return inconsistent data, so the rate is tuned rather than maximized. For the defender, the lesson is to assume IPs are shared and enumerable and to treat 'unlisted' as meaningless \u2014 anything that answers on an in-range address is discoverable and must be inventoried and hardened accordingly.",w5l3:"Once a target's addresses and names are mapped, the next question is the literal listening surface: which ports respond and what runs behind them. An open port is a service, and a service is code that will hold a conversation with anyone who connects. Conceptually this proceeds in two stages: a mass port scanner sweeps large IP ranges quickly to find what is listening, and an HTTP prober then determines which of those listeners are actually web applications worth deeper attention.\n\nThe decisive skill is not operating the tools but interpreting their output. A 200 versus a 401 versus a connection reset, a server banner, a redirect to a login page \u2014 each signal narrows thousands of open ports to the few that are genuinely interesting. The tooling produces data; the analyst produces the shortlist, and the quality of the engagement is mostly the quality of that reduction.\n\nScanning is also a continuous tradeoff between rate and breadth on one side and stealth and accuracy on the other. Push too hard and you trip rate limits and detection or simply collect inconsistent results; move too cautiously and the sweep never completes. The scan is tuned to the estate. For defenders the implication is blunt: everything that listens is enumerable from outside, so the real defense is to listen less \u2014 close unneeded ports, gate services behind authentication and allowlists, and never treat an obscure port as protection.",w5l4:"There is a clean line in recon between knowing that a service is present and knowing exactly what it is. Crossing it is the pivot of the whole discipline. An open service is only potential, but a precisely versioned service \u2014 a specific web server release, a specific CMS version \u2014 maps directly to that release's known vulnerabilities, letting an attacker skip straight to what is already broken.\n\nVersions leak through many channels: Server and X-Powered-By headers, default error pages, characteristic file paths, JavaScript bundle hashes, and subtle behavioral differences between releases. Forcing a service into an error frequently discloses more than its normal responses do. The pivot itself is mechanical: fingerprint the product and pin the exact release, then map that release to public vulnerability data such as CVEs and vendor advisories. Recon ends precisely where 'which known issues apply here' begins.\n\nPrecision cuts both ways. A wrong version wastes effort on exploits that cannot apply, and defenders sometimes strip or falsify version strings, so a banner should be corroborated with behavior before it is trusted. The defensive takeaway is the mirror image of the technique: do not advertise versions. Strip identifying headers, normalize error pages, and patch, because the entire method lives in the gap between the moment a version is disclosed and the moment a patch is applied.",w6l1:"Subdomains multiply the attack surface because each one is typically a distinct host running a distinct application \u2014 staging, admin, legacy, vendor integrations \u2014 and organizations create them far more readily than they retire them. The subdomain tree is therefore where forgotten and weaker applications accumulate, which is exactly where an attacker wants to look.\n\nDiscovery divides into two modes. Passive enumeration mines records that already exist elsewhere \u2014 Certificate Transparency logs, search engines, public DNS datasets \u2014 without ever sending a packet to the target. Active enumeration queries DNS directly, brute-forcing candidate names to learn which resolve. Certificate Transparency deserves special mention: because every issued TLS certificate is written to a public log, subdomain names (including internal-sounding ones) become public the instant a certificate is issued, with no scanning required.\n\nThe two modes trade off against each other. Passive is stealthy, fast, and broad, but it can only surface what has been recorded somewhere; active finds names that were never published anywhere but is noisier and touches the target's resolvers. Serious recon runs both and merges the results. For defenders the consequence is stark: assume every subdomain you have ever certified is public knowledge, inventory the whole tree, and retire dead entries \u2014 a forgotten dev subdomain is a production-grade entry point.",w6l2:"Active subdomain enumeration is brute force against DNS: a wordlist of candidate names is resolved to see which exist. The available tooling is fast, but speed is not the difficult part \u2014 trusting the answers is, because DNS will readily return results that are not real.\n\nThe canonical trap is wildcard DNS. A domain configured with a wildcard record resolves anything.target.com, so a naive brute force reports millions of subdomains that do not exist. Sound enumeration detects the wildcard and discards names that resolve only because of it. Resolvers introduce a second class of error: public resolvers can be poisoned, rate-limited, or simply return junk, so results are validated against a vetted resolver list (the role of a tool such as DNSValidator) and confirmed hits are re-checked. A name counts as real only when trusted resolvers agree it is.\n\nThe deliverable of this stage is therefore not 'names that returned an answer' but 'names confirmed to exist after wildcard and resolver validation.' Skipping that step floods the engagement with false positives that waste every subsequent stage. For defenders, the takeaway is that brute force reliably finds predictable names like dev, vpn, and jira, so obscure naming provides no protection; the only real control is ensuring that every name which resolves points to something inventoried and hardened.",w6l3:"The most valuable subdomains are often absent from any wordlist because they leak directly from an application's own configuration. A Content Security Policy header, in order to function, enumerates the domains a site trusts for scripts, frames, and connections \u2014 effectively a curated list of related hosts published in plain sight. Favicon hashing approaches the problem from the opposite direction: hashing a site's favicon and querying internet-wide scan datasets for every host serving the same icon ties together an organization's scattered or deliberately differently-named assets.\n\nThese techniques, combined with passive sources and active brute force, produce overlapping and inconsistent lists, which is why aggregation tools such as Amass exist \u2014 they fuse many sources into a single normalized, deduplicated set of real hosts. Consolidation is genuine analytical work rather than a formatting step: the same host appears under multiple names, with and without ports, behind CDNs and redirect chains, and reducing all of that to distinct, live assets is what converts raw output into a workable target list.\n\nFor defenders, the uncomfortable point is that CSP headers and favicons are recon signals that cannot really be hidden, so the defense is upstream: keep the asset inventory tight, because anything that points back to the organization \u2014 a trusted domain in a policy, a shared icon \u2014 is a thread an attacker will pull.",w6l4:"Subdomain takeover is frequently the payoff of thorough enumeration. It arises when a DNS record \u2014 most often a CNAME \u2014 still points to a third-party service such as a cloud storage bucket, a platform-as-a-service application, or a SaaS page that has been deleted or was never claimed. The dangling record is the vulnerability: DNS asserts that blog.target.com is a particular hosted app, but the app no longer exists and the underlying name is free to register.\n\nClaiming that resource gives the attacker control of what loads at the subdomain, and because the subdomain is genuinely part of the victim's domain, the impact is same-site by definition. That enables phishing on a trusted name, capture of cookies scoped to the parent domain, satisfaction of OAuth and redirect allowlists, and bypass of any control that trusts '*.target.com'. It is not a lookalike domain; it is the real subdomain.\n\nConfirmation is careful, non-destructive work. A CNAME pointing at an unclaimed service usually exhibits a provider-specific tell \u2014 a particular 404 or a 'no such app' fingerprint \u2014 and the tester verifies that the record is genuinely dangling and the resource is claimable rather than defacing a live service. For defenders, takeover is fundamentally a lifecycle bug: when a cloud resource is torn down, its DNS record must be removed first, and standing CNAMEs pointing at services the organization no longer owns should be audited, because each dangling record is a trusted-origin host waiting to be claimed.",w7l1:"The pages a site links to are only the portion it intends to present. The real attack surface includes everything that responds to a request but is not linked anywhere: administrative panels, superseded API versions, backup archives, debug and health endpoints, and leftover test pages. Directory and content fuzzing surfaces these by brute force \u2014 requesting a wordlist of likely paths such as /admin, /api/v1, /.git, or /backup.zip and observing which return something other than 'not found.'\n\nThe technique is productive precisely because unlinked endpoints are so often the unprotected ones. A developer who 'hides' an administrative route by not linking it frequently never applies authentication to it, treating obscurity as a control; fuzzing dismantles that assumption in seconds. As with scanning, interpreting the output is the real skill \u2014 distinguishing a genuine 200 from a soft-404 that returns 200 with an error body, recognizing a 403 as proof that a path exists but is gated, and following redirects. Status and body together establish whether a result is a real discovery.\n\nThe defensive conclusion is unambiguous: assume every path is reachable and enumerable. Declining to link a route protects nothing, because the route still answers; the only effective protection is authentication and authorization enforced on the route itself, applied uniformly across the application.",w7l2:"If endpoints are doors, parameters are the questions asked through them, and each parameter \u2014 whether in the query string, request body, a header, a cookie, or a path segment \u2014 is a distinct point at which attacker input meets application logic, and therefore a distinct place a vulnerability can live. Applications routinely honor parameters that appear in no documentation: debug flags, feature toggles, legacy fields the code still reads, and alternate names a framework happens to accept. Parameter discovery brute-forces and infers these so that the tester exercises inputs the developers have forgotten remain live.\n\nCrawling and spidering tools such as Gospider construct the link graph by following hyperlinks, forms, and URLs referenced from JavaScript, converting an opaque site into an indexed set of endpoints and the parameters they carry. Because applications change with every deploy \u2014 a new endpoint here, a new API referenced by an updated bundle there \u2014 surface mapping is run continuously rather than once, so that surface appearing after the initial pass is still caught.\n\nThe defensive point follows directly: every parameter the code reads is attack surface regardless of whether it is documented, and dead or legacy parameters the code still honors are pure liability. If it is parsed, it will be tested, so unused inputs should be removed rather than left quietly accepted.",w7l3:"Effective testing begins by identifying what an application is built from \u2014 the web server, the application framework, the CMS, the front-end libraries, and any web application firewall in front of it \u2014 because the stack dictates which classes of bug are plausible and which known issues are worth checking first. Technology fingerprinting reads the tells a stack cannot help emitting: response headers, cookie names (a framework's default session cookie is a giveaway), default error pages, characteristic file paths, and front-end asset hashes, with tools such as WhatWeb and Wappalyzer automating the matching.\n\nForcing errors is the highest-yield technique, because a malformed request or a request for a non-existent resource frequently returns a stack trace, a version string, or a framework's distinctive error page that the normal happy path conceals. Fingerprinting is a focusing step rather than an end in itself: establishing that a target is a specific CMS at a specific version behind a specific WAF tells the tester which payloads to attempt, which known issues apply, and which evasions will be required, turning a broad target into a concrete plan.\n\nDefensively, the goal is to minimize what the stack announces \u2014 stripping framework headers and telltale cookies, replacing default error pages, and avoiding version-specific paths \u2014 because every tell removed forces an attacker to guess where they could otherwise simply know.",w7l4:"The final stage of reconnaissance before bespoke testing is to check everything discovered against everything already known to be broken. Templated vulnerability scanners, of which Nuclei is the archetype, run a large library of signatures \u2014 one per known issue \u2014 across the entire list of discovered hosts. Each template encodes a known issue as a request-and-match: send a particular request, look for a particular response, and thereby flag an exposed administrative panel, a default credential, a specific CVE, an information leak, or a misconfiguration. This is the version-to-CVE pivot from Week 5, automated across the whole estate.\n\nThe value lies in scale and freshness: thousands of checks against thousands of hosts, with the template set updated as new issues are published, so that what was once a manual 'is this the vulnerable version?' becomes an immediate sweep for the low-hanging fruit. The unavoidable caveat is false positives. A signature can match coincidentally, and a flagged issue may be patched while still fingerprinting as vulnerable, or simply be wrong. Reconnaissance flags candidates; the tester confirms before claiming, treating a finding as a lead rather than a conclusion.\n\nThe defensive mirror is sobering: attackers run the same public templates against an organization the day a CVE is disclosed, so the exposure window is the gap between disclosure and patching. Running the same scanners inwardly and treating any 'exposed and known-vulnerable' result as already under attack is the only way to close that window first.",w8l1:"Cloud object storage \u2014 Amazon S3 buckets and their equivalents on other platforms \u2014 occupies a single global namespace populated with human-predictable names such as companyname-backups, companyname-assets, and companyname-dev. Predictability makes the namespace enumerable, and enumeration proceeds two ways: guessing from wordlists seeded with the organization's name and common suffixes, and harvesting bucket references the application leaks through asset URLs in HTML and JavaScript, redirects, error messages, and mobile app bundles.\n\nA bucket's existence can be confirmed even without read access, because the provider returns distinct responses for a name that does not exist versus one that exists but denies access. Enumeration therefore establishes which candidate names are real before any attempt to read their contents. This matters because buckets routinely store backups, credentials, customer data, and build artifacts; a single over-permissive bucket can disclose far more than any vulnerability in the application itself, and it frequently sits entirely outside the app's own security boundary.\n\nThe defensive posture follows directly: treat bucket names as discoverable rather than secret, derive all security from the access policy rather than from name obscurity, and avoid leaking bucket references in client-side code where harvesting them is trivial.",w8l2:"A bucket is exactly as safe as the access configuration attached to it \u2014 the ACL and the bucket policy that define who may read, write, and list its contents. Misconfiguration here is the entire vulnerability, because the data is only ever one permission away from public. Three holes recur: public read, which lets anyone download the contents; public list, which lets anyone enumerate every object key and so converts 'I need the filename' into a complete index; and public write, which lets anyone replace objects.\n\nPublic write is the case practitioners most often underrate. If a site serves its JavaScript or other assets from a bucket and that bucket is world-writable, an attacker who overwrites those assets obtains stored cross-site scripting on every page that loads them \u2014 a storage misconfiguration escalating directly into a supply-chain compromise. Identifying these holes is a matter of reasoning rather than tooling: a policy can permit an action through an overly broad principal, a wildcard resource, or a missing condition, and the policy is read with the same question used for every access-control bug \u2014 what does this permit that it should not?\n\nDefensively the rules are familiar: default-deny, least privilege, block public access at the account level, and rigorously separate read from write. Above all, never serve executable assets from a bucket that anyone can write to.",w8l3:"One cloud misconfiguration is subtle enough to look careful while being catastrophic: granting a bucket, or any resource, to the provider's 'Authenticated Users' group. The label suggests restriction \u2014 access requires being logged in \u2014 but on the major platforms it means any account holder on the entire platform, not anyone within the organization. Because anyone can create a free account in minutes, 'Authenticated Users' is effectively public with one trivial extra step.\n\nThe underlying error is the conflation of authentication with authorization. Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides permission; granting to 'Authenticated Users' answers the first question when it should answer the second. This is not a niche cloud quirk but a specific instance of a general root cause that recurs throughout the course \u2014 scoping access to 'is logged in' rather than 'is allowed to do this.' Exploitation is mundane by design: register for the platform, present valid credentials, and the resource treats the attacker as trusted, with no cryptographic flaw or crafted payload involved.\n\nThe defensive rule is correspondingly simple and absolute: never grant to broad, platform-wide groups. Scope grants to specific principals \u2014 named roles and accounts \u2014 and specific actions, and read every grant as authorization to perform exactly one thing, never as a welcome to any party that happens to be authenticated.",w8l4:"The recon half of Semester 1 is best understood not as a collection of tools but as a single compounding pipeline. Ownership data \u2014 ASNs and announced IP blocks \u2014 establishes the scope. Names (subdomains) and hosts (open ports and services) populate that scope. Surface mapping (endpoints and parameters) and fingerprinting describe each application in turn. Templated scanning flags whatever is already known to be broken, and cloud enumeration captures the assets that live outside the application entirely. Each stage produces the input to the next.\n\nThe compounding is the point. A subdomain discovered in Week 6 becomes a host to scan; its service version pivots to a known issue; its unlinked admin panel was surfaced by fuzzing; its referenced bucket was enumerated and its policy read. The value of reconnaissance lies in these connections rather than in any single finding. Three disciplines keep the pipeline honest across all of it: breadth, because the forgotten asset is so often the way in; validation, because wildcards, false positives, and dangling records all lie; and prioritization, because the highest-impact, most-exposed surface should be worked first.\n\nReconnaissance ends where exploitation begins, with a validated, prioritized map of the target's real attack surface \u2014 annotated with stack, versions, exposed endpoints, and known issues. That map is precisely the input to Semester 2, where the work shifts from describing the surface to turning it into access. The strongest defense is to run this entire pipeline inwardly and continuously, because whoever maps the surface first sets the terms.",w9l1:"SQL injection is the canonical instance of the master vulnerability behind this entire semester: an application constructs a database query by concatenating untrusted input into the query string, and the database parser cannot distinguish the developer's intended structure from the attacker's injected syntax. Input crosses from the data plane, where it is merely a value, into the code plane, where it is executed as part of the query.\n\nThe mechanism is visible in a single line. Given \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '\" + input + \"'\", an input of ' OR '1'='1 closes the string literal and adds logic the developer never wrote; the parser honors it because, at the byte level, the attacker's characters are indistinguishable from legitimate SQL. This is not a peculiarity of SQL. The same shape recurs wherever a value is concatenated into a language the receiver will parse \u2014 SQL, operating-system commands, template expressions, NoSQL queries \u2014 and is the unifying idea of the week.\n\nA working classification helps organize the techniques that follow: in-band injection returns the answer in the response (through errors or UNION); blind injection returns nothing directly and must be inferred bit by bit; and second-order injection stores the payload now and executes it later, at a distant sink. The remedy is not more aggressive escaping but the elimination of plane-mixing altogether: parameterized queries and prepared statements transmit the query structure and the data on separate channels, so bound input is always a value and can never be reparsed as syntax. Escaping patches symptoms; separation cures the disease.",w9l2:"When a vulnerable query's results are rendered in the response, UNION-based extraction is the most direct technique. Appending a UNION SELECT welds a second query onto the first, so the database returns attacker-chosen data alongside, or in place of, the intended rows. UNION imposes two preconditions that shape the opening moves: the two SELECT statements must return the same number of columns, and the columns the attacker reads must hold compatible types. The first task is therefore to determine the original query's column count \u2014 commonly by incrementing an ORDER BY until it errors \u2014 and to identify which columns are actually displayed.\n\nWith the shape aligned, the attacker selects from anything reachable: directly from a table such as users, or, more powerfully, from the database's own metadata catalog to enumerate table and column names and map the schema before dumping it. Because the data returns in the response, this is in-band exfiltration \u2014 fast and unambiguous \u2014 and it is the technique of choice whenever results are reflected. When they are not, UNION offers nothing and the tester falls back to blind inference.\n\nDefensively, UNION extraction is only a symptom; the disease is the unparameterized query. Parameterization removes the injection point entirely, while least-privilege database accounts and a discipline of not reflecting raw query output limit the damage if an injection slips through.",w9l3:"Frequently an injection succeeds but returns no data, because the query executes server-side and only its effect is observable. Blind SQL injection extracts data regardless, by converting the database into an oracle that answers yes/no questions one at a time. In the boolean-based variant, the attacker injects a condition and observes the response: a test such as AND SUBSTRING(password,1,1)='a' makes the page render normally when true and differently when false, so iterating over candidate characters and positions reconstructs the secret one character per request.\n\nWhen the response does not change at all, the time-based variant induces a measurable delay instead: a construct like AND IF(condition, SLEEP(5), 0) causes a slow response precisely when the condition holds, so the secret is read through a stopwatch. Both variants are inference rather than retrieval \u2014 the attacker never sees the data directly but deduces it from a side channel, whether a content difference or a timing difference. The process is tedious by hand and automated in practice, but the essential concept is that any observable difference suffices to build a boolean oracle.\n\nThe defensive lesson is that withholding query results is not a protection, because the application's behavior leaks the information anyway. The only real fix remains parameterization; uniform responses and constant-time handling merely slow the attacker rather than closing the bug.",w9l4:"First-order injection executes within the request that carries the payload. Second-order injection is more subtle: the payload is stored as ordinary data on the way in, then later read back and concatenated into a different query, where it finally executes. The vulnerable sink is nowhere near the source, which is exactly why the bug evades naive defenses. A classic shape is registering a username such as admin'-- that is stored without incident, only to detonate later when an administrative report builds a query from stored usernames by concatenation. Entry-point validation misses it because, at the point of entry, the value was never used as code.\n\nThis generalizes the hardest idea in the course: the dangerous moment is where data reaches a sink, not where it enters the system. Securing the front door is insufficient; a value must be traced to every place it is subsequently used, because any unparameterized use is a potential detonation point.\n\nThe impact ceiling is also higher than data theft. Depending on the engine and configuration, stacked queries can run additional statements, some databases can read and write files, and batch or command features can reach the operating system, escalating a query bug into remote code execution. The defensive response is twofold: parameterize every query \u2014 including the internal ones that consume already-stored data, which teams routinely overlook \u2014 and run the database under a least-privilege account with file and command capabilities disabled, so that even a missed injection cannot reach the host."}},499,[]);
